FGA_CHECK:212-5.2	2026-11-18 20:19:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT Pro mode, this run	independent re-proof	Check surjectivity on geometric points. Suppose two Omega-valued points of X over the same point of Y, with Omega algebraically closed, are in different relation orbits. The outgoing-arrow fibre is finite, so its orbit is finite. In B tensor_A Omega, the distinct Omega-points have distinct maximal ideals; Chinese remainders provide u vanishing at the second point and nonzero at every point of the first orbit. Write u as a finite sum of b_i with scalar coefficients c_i. Form the norm of p2 prime(sum b_i T_i) universally over B[T_i]. Its coefficients lie in A by the composition argument, because polynomial extension is flat and the groupoid acts on the outgoing arrows preserving targets. Specializing T_i to c_i therefore gives the same norm value at the two Omega-points. At the second it is zero by the identity section; at the first it is nonzero, since the corresponding element of its finite Omega-algebra is a unit. Contradiction. Thus every geometric pair has an arrow, proving R1 to X times_Y X surjective. This argument does not assume invariants commute with arbitrary, possibly nonflat, base change.	212, p. 111, Corollary 5.2	french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/FGA_CHECK_checkpoint_12_20261119T0612Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
