FGA_CHECK:212-8-GL	2026-11-18 20:31:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT Pro mode, this run	independent re-proof	For n at least one, GL(n) is represented by Spec Z[x_ij,det(x)^(-1)]. Giving a morphism from any scheme T to this affine scheme is giving global sections x_ij with invertible determinant, exactly an invertible n by n matrix over Gamma(T,O_T). Matrix multiplication and the adjugate-over-determinant inverse define its group structure. Tensoring the coordinate ring with a base ring, or taking its relative Spec, proves arbitrary base compatibility. For n=1 this is Spec Z[t,t^(-1)], representing global units. The implicit positive-rank convention is left unchanged in the historical wording.	212, p. 116, matrix and multiplicative group construction	french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/FGA_CHECK_checkpoint_12_20261119T0612Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
FGA_CHECK:212-8-projective-group	2026-11-18 20:31:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT Pro mode, this run	independent re-proof	Let P be projective space on the n squared matrix entries over Z. The open D_+(det) represents matrices up to invertible scalar locally on the base. GL(n) to this open is a Zariski locally trivial G_m-torsor: on an open where an entry is a generator, divide all entries by that entry. Matrix multiplication and adjugate inversion are independent of the chosen local scalar and glue to a group structure. Thus this open represents the Zariski sheaf quotient of GL(n) by scalars, with the notation GP(n-1) used by the print. It is affine because D_+(det) is a standard affine open of Proj. This directly verifies the sheaf-of-sections description without replacing it by the generally incorrect quotient of global sections.	212, p. 116, definition of GP(n-1)	french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/FGA_CHECK_checkpoint_12_20261119T0612Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
FGA_CHECK:212-8-projective-lifting	2026-11-18 20:31:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT Pro mode, this run	independent re-proof	Explicit nonlifting example for n=2: take R=Z[s]/(s^2+5), and I=(2,1+s). One has I^2=(2): the generators 4,2+2s,-4+2s are multiples of 2 and their combinations contain 2. Hence I is invertible. It is not principal: R/I has two elements, whereas a principal generator a+b s would have index a^2+5b^2, which cannot equal two. The matrix with rows (2,1+s) and (1-s,2) has all entries in I and determinant -2, a generator of I^2. It therefore gives an isomorphism R^2 to I direct-sum I. Trivializing I locally turns it into invertible matrices differing on overlaps by scalar units, hence a section of GP(1). A global GL(2) lift would trivialize the associated scalar line bundle I, contradiction. This checks the printed warning that projective sections need not lift globally.	212, p. 116, parenthetical lifting warning	french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/FGA_CHECK_checkpoint_12_20261119T0612Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
