ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.1.5.2-resolution-formula-types	2026-11-18 14:41:55 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted type check	Write Q=TU and R=UT. The displayed face Q^i*b*Q^(n-i) has Q^(n+1)->Q^n; the degeneracy Q^i*T*a*U*Q^(n-i) has Q^(n+1)->Q^(n+2). The coface R^i*a*R^(n-i+1) has R^(n+1)->R^(n+2), and codegeneracy R^i*U*b*T*R^(n-i-1) has R^(n+1)->R^n. All four interval endpoints therefore match the stated degree conventions, including the unit/counit at degree -1. This checks source/target exponents and boundary typing, not every simplicial identity.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.1.5.4-homotopy-endpoints	2026-11-18 14:46:13 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Using the already read convention of 1.1.5, d^0 epsilon is constant 1 and d^1 epsilon is constant 0. For u(f)=f_1^bullet f_(1 bullet), constant 1 makes f_1=id_E and gives Id, while constant 0 makes f_1 the empty inclusion and gives phi^bullet phi_bullet. Thus u has the printed endpoint order Id then phi^bullet phi_bullet. For v using f_0, constant 1 gives phi^bullet phi_bullet and constant 0 gives Id, again the printed order. The composites are endomorphisms because f_(j bullet) goes from the E-value to the fiber-value and f_j^bullet returns. This checks typing and endpoints only; general naturality and the complete homotopy-triviality theorem have not been independently proved here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.1.5.5-composed-adjunction	2026-11-18 14:53:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Using the displayed adjunctions, Hom_{A-Alg}(S_A(A^(X)),B) is naturally Hom_{A-Mod}(A^(X),underlying B), which is naturally Hom_T(X,underlying B). With the definition A[X]=S_A(A^(X)), this gives (1.5.5.5) and forces a separator between its source arguments A[X] and B. This verifies the composition and typing of the stated adjunction, not an independent construction of the free-module or symmetric-algebra adjunctions in an arbitrary topos.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.1.1-relation-reflexivity	2026-11-18 15:02:32 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	For n >= 1, the printed i=n instance together with d_n z=u forces u=s_{n-1}d_nu. In the Kan nerve of a nontrivial group, take n=1 and u=v a nonidentity arrow: s_0d_1u is the identity arrow, so no printed witness exists and the purported equivalence relation is not reflexive. With the strict bound i<n, z=s_nu gives d_nz=d_{n+1}z=u and d_i z=s_{n-1}d_i u for every i<n by the simplicial identities. This verifies the necessity of removing the endpoint and the corrected reflexivity test, not a complete proof of the Kan homotopy-relation theorem or the Ex construction.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.1.1-NX-object-type	2026-11-18 15:06:42 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Let X be the constant simplicial group on a nontrivial group G. All faces and degeneracies are identity maps, so the displayed definition gives NX_0=G (empty intersection) and NX_1=ker(d_1)=1. Consequently s_0(NX_0) is not contained in NX_1, disproving the claim that NX is a simplicial subgroup. In general, d_i d_0=d_0 d_{i+1} for i>0 shows d_0(NX_n) lies in NX_{n-1}, and d_0d_0=d_0d_1 gives trivial composite on NX_n for n>=2. Thus the positive-face kernels with d_0 form the normal complex of groups; this is the minimal terminology from the already read (1.3.1). No claim of a full independent proof of the Moore homotopy theorem.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.1.1-Ex-and-basepoint-types	2026-11-18 15:06:42 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	For f:[0,m]->[0,n], Sd Delta(f):Sd Delta(m)->Sd Delta(n); precomposition therefore maps Hom(Sd Delta(n),Y) to Hom(Sd Delta(m),Y), as required by simplicial variance. Precomposition by delta:Sd Delta(m)->Delta(m) maps Y_m=Hom(Delta(m),Y) to Ex(Y)_m. The recursive composite e_{Ex^n(X)} e_X^n consequently has source X and target Ex^{n+1}(X). Finally right multiplication by x^{-1}y sends x to y and has inverse right multiplication by y^{-1}x, verifying the printed base-point change and its direction. This checks typing and the explicit translation, not the Kan or Ex weak-equivalence theorems.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.1.1-boundary-normality	2026-11-18 15:14:18 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	For a in (NX)_n and b in (NX)_{n+1}, set c=(s_0 a)b(s_0 a)^{-1}. The simplicial identities give d_1 c=a1a^{-1}=1 and, for i>1, d_i s_0 a=s_0 d_{i-1}a=1 and d_i b=1. Thus c belongs to (NX)_{n+1}, and d_0 c=a(d_0 b)a^{-1}. Therefore the boundary image is normal even in (NX)_n, hence in its cycle subgroup. For n>=1, d_0d_0b=d_0d_1b=1 gives the required containment in the cycle subgroup. This verifies that the displayed homology quotient is defined in positive degrees; it does not independently prove its identification with homotopy groups.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.1.2-NX-object-type-and-footnote	2026-11-18 15:16:37 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Specialize T to the topos of sets and X to the constant simplicial object on a nontrivial group G. Then NX_0=G and NX_1=1 while s_0=id, so the repeated claim that NX is a simplicial subgroup is false even in this special case. The differential identities checked for p22 apply sectionwise, giving the normal complex. For the footnote, a section U->X induces f^*U->f^*X by functoriality, and the base section x induces f^*x because f^* preserves the final object. This verifies the direction and typing of the sectionwise pointed comparison, without asserting it is an isomorphism before homotopy-sheaf formation.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.1-cone-cutoff	2026-11-18 15:18:55 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Put m=-n and regard the normalized homological complexes cohomologically, so H^j=H_{-j}. The n-quasi-isomorphism condition gives an isomorphism H^j(f) for j>m and an epimorphism for j=m. In the cone exact sequence H^j(X)->H^j(Y)->H^j(Cone(f))->H^{j+1}(X)->H^{j+1}(Y), these imply H^j(Cone(f))=0 for j>=m. Conversely, that vanishing gives surjectivity at m and, using the preceding cone term, injectivity as well for j>m. This verifies the printed >=-n cutoff and its equivalence to the stated abelian condition, conditional on the homotopy/homology identification already quoted.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.3-corollary-and-LF-types	2026-11-18 15:22:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Assuming Theorem 2.2.2, a quasi-isomorphism f satisfies its n-quasi-isomorphism hypothesis for every n>=2. For any homotopy degree k>=0 choose n>=max(2,k+1); the conclusion makes pi_k(Z^(f)) an isomorphism. Thus the n>=2 restriction causes no gap in Corollary 2.2.3. Separately, an arrow V->U of T is U->V in T^o, so a cofibered category gives L(U)->L(V) as printed. In LF2 both restricted arrows have target j|U_alpha, exactly the stated source of s_alpha, so both composites are defined. This checks the corollary conditional on its theorem and the local axiom types, not a new proof of Whitehead's theorem.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.5-cofinal-locally-filtered	2026-11-18 15:32:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	For a full cofibered cofinal L-prime in L, LF0 for L provides local objects; cofinality refines the covering to objects of L-prime. For LF1, obtain a common target k in L for two L-prime objects after a cover, then refine by cofinality to k->k-prime in L-prime; composites belong to L-prime by fullness. For LF2, equalize a parallel pair by postcomposition into k in L, then refine to k->k-prime in L-prime; equality survives composition and fullness supplies the arrow in L-prime. Cofibered restriction stays in L-prime and composite covers are covers. Thus L-prime is locally filtered. This does not independently prove the site-equivalence or local-colimit cofinality theorem. Lemma 2.2.6 remains mid-proof at the page boundary.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.6-fiber-filtered	2026-11-18 15:36:40 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	Fix U. The zero complex-arrow mapping to u(U) gives an object of L(U). Finite direct sums of two complex-arrows, with the induced map to u(U), give a common target. For parallel arrows f,g from i to j over u(U), take their componentwise cokernel in the arrow category of complexes; the structure map to u(U) factors because it annihilates f-g. A cokernel of a map between modules of finite presentation has finite presentation: take a finite presentation of the target and append the images of finitely many generators of the source as relations. Thus every component of the coequalizer remains of finite presentation, proving each L(U) filtered. Restriction maps L(U)->L(V) and the given cofibered construction then satisfy the local filteredness axioms with trivial covers. This checks the filteredness assertion, not the colimit-isomorphism proof continuing on p30.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.6-condition-a-disk	2026-11-18 15:39:11 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	For x in E^n(U), let D have A in degrees n,n+1, identity differential between them and zero elsewhere. Set E_i=F_i=D and u_i=Id. Map E_i to E(U) by x in degree n and dx in degree n+1; d(dx)=0 makes this a chain map. Map F_i to F(U) by u(U)x and d u(U)x. The equality d u(U)x=u(U)dx follows because u is a chain map, so the cube gives an object i in L(U). Taking y=1 in E_i^n=A gives i(y)=x and proves condition (a), even without a cover. This checks the displayed construction and its eight vertices/twelve edge types; condition (b) and the full conclusion remain on the next page.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.6-condition-b	2026-11-18 15:42:28 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	If z in E_i^n maps to zero, quotient E_i by the subcomplex generated by z and dz, and F_i by that generated by u_i z and d u_i z. The structural maps to E(U),F(U) kill these subcomplexes, so descend to an object j of L(U); i->j kills z. Each affected submodule is finitely generated, and a quotient of a module of finite presentation by a finitely generated submodule has finite presentation by adjoining finitely many relations. For a representative in F_i, quotient only F_i by the analogous generated subcomplex, leaving E_i unchanged. No localization is needed. Together with the disk construction on p30 (for the F case use 0->D), this verifies both local representation and zero criteria for the componentwise associated-sheaf comparison in Lemma 2.2.6.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.2.2.6.1-bounded-cofinality	2026-11-18 15:42:28 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof check	For any complex C_i mapping into a complex supported on [a,b], form its quotient truncation: zero below a, coker(d:C_i^(a-1)->C_i^a) at a, unchanged in degrees a<k<=b, and zero above b. The differential is well-defined by d squared zero, and the original map factors since its target vanishes outside [a,b]. The projection is a chain map, and all components retain finite presentation because the only new component is a cokernel between modules of finite presentation. Apply this functor to both source and target of u_i. It gives i->j in the specified full subsystem without a cover, establishing the stated cofinality. Omit the respective truncation when an endpoint is infinite.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
