ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.2-variable-ring-and-A-homotopy	2026-11-18 17:12:07 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	A map (A,E)->(B,F) consists of a ring map A->B and an A-linear map E->F with F restricted along that map. The tensor extension/restriction adjunction gives the equivalent B-linear map E tensor_A B->F. Degreewise simplicial compatibility identifies simplicial objects in this category with modules over variable simplicial rings, as in the already read (1.1.6). Under the simplicial exponential adjunction, a homotopy over the identity of A is a map E->internal Homs(Delta(1),F) that is A-linear for the constant-path A action: evaluation at every simplicial parameter respects the same scalar. Formula (3.1.1.7) then identifies it with an A-linear map E tensor_A A^(Delta(1))->F, with endpoints induced by the two vertices of Delta(1). This checks the stated interpretation without asserting a result about the still-unread following formula.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.2.1-simplicial-abelian-group	2026-11-18 17:16:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, counterexample and type check	Let G=Homs_A(E,F), the simplicial abelian group of global A-linear maps. Its vertices are ordinary A-linear maps and its edges are elementary A-homotopies. For an edge h with d0h=f,d1h=g, the edge -h+s0f+s0g reverses its endpoints. If k has endpoints g,r, then h+k-s0g has endpoints f,r; s0f supplies reflexivity. Thus elementary homotopy is already an equivalence relation in the module case, and its quotient is pi_0(G)=G_0/im(d0-d1), an abelian group. This checks (3.1.2.1) and explains why its Homs must be plain. It does not assert that global sections commute with sheaf pi_0.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.2-original-fiber-counterexample	2026-11-18 17:16:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, counterexample and type check	For T=Ens, Alg(T) as introduced in the preceding paragraph is the arrow category of commutative rings. Its strict fiber under (source,target) over (A,B) has objects ring maps A->B and only identity arrows: a morphism in that fiber has both components identity, forcing the two maps to coincide. With A=B=Z there is exactly one object, id_Z. In contrast Z-Alg/Z contains the augmented algebra Z[t] with t mapped to 0, and the endomorphism t->0 is a nonidentity map over Z. Hence the asserted fiber cannot be Z-Alg/Z, even up to equivalence. The same counterexample uses constant simplicial objects. This is a defect in the original, distinct from the supplied transcription errors.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.2-corrected-factorization-fibration	2026-11-18 17:16:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, counterexample and type check	Take the category whose objects are factorizations A0->P0->B0 of ring maps and whose arrows are commutative diagrams. Send a factorization to its composite A0->B0, an object of Alg(T). Given a square (a:A-prime->A,b:B-prime->B) between composites, pull back A->P->B to A-prime->P times_B B-prime->B-prime; the first map has components i a and the prescribed A-prime->B-prime, compatible by the square. The universal property of the pullback gives a cartesian lift, so this composition functor is fibered. Simplicial objects in the factorization category over the fixed simplicial ring map A->B are exactly diagrams A->P->B with that composite and maps fixing A and B: the category A-Alg/B. This verifies every part of the replacement formulation.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.2-path-localization-universal-property	2026-11-18 17:16:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, counterexample and type check	For modules, A-algebras, and A-algebras over B, the appropriate path object is the internal Homs(Delta(1),Q), or its relative version over B. Constant paths c:Q->P(Q) are homotopy equivalences: endpoint evaluation is a retraction and interval contraction gives the other composite homotopic to the identity. Precomposition by the interval contraction preserves the pointwise algebra operations, the constant-path A action, and the constant B image in the relative case. Since d0 c=d1 c=Id, any functor inverting these homotopy equivalences identifies d0 and d1, hence identifies a homotopic pair represented by u:P->P(Q). Conversely the homotopy quotient inverts homotopy equivalences by their defining inverses. The universal properties therefore identify each indicated Hot category with the localization. The elementary homotopy relation is proved transitive above for modules only; no analogous unproved assertion about arbitrary algebra mapping spaces is used.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.3-shuffle-DG-structure	2026-11-18 17:18:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Compose the shuffle chain map (1.2.2.1), A-tilde tensor_Z A-tilde -> (A tensor_Z A)-tilde, with simplicial multiplication. Associativity follows because both parenthesizations enumerate the same ordered three-block shuffles; the permutation signs multiply to the same total sign. The chain identity groups faces of shuffle terms: mixed adjacent-block faces cancel with the shuffle obtained by exchanging those adjacent entries; the remaining faces are the terms of dx*y and (-1)^p x*dy. Thus multiplication satisfies the graded Leibniz rule. The degree-zero unit is the simplicial ring unit. Replacing the final multiplication by A times M -> M yields the differential graded module action and the same associative and Leibniz identities.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.3-strict-anticommutativity	2026-11-18 17:18:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For degrees p,q, exchange the two blocks in each (p,q)-shuffle. This bijects the indexing sets and changes the permutation sign by (-1)^(pq). The degeneracy maps are ring maps and A is commutative under the p55 convention, so the corresponding products agree after exchanging factors. Therefore x*y=(-1)^(pq)y*x. If x has odd degree p>0, the complementary-block involution on (p,p)-shuffles has no fixed point; paired products are identical with opposite signs. They cancel integrally, proving x*x=0 even in the presence of 2-torsion. This is stronger than merely deriving 2x*x=0 from graded commutativity.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.3-divided-power-combinatorics	2026-11-18 17:18:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For a positive even homogeneous x of degree p, the n-fold shuffle product is indexed by ordered partitions into n blocks of size p. Permuting whole blocks has even sign and leaves the product of the identical x factors unchanged. Summing once per unordered block orbit defines gamma_n(x) integrally and canonically; n! gamma_n(x)=x^n is a consequence, not division by n!. Counting choices of groups of blocks gives gamma_r(x) gamma_s(x)=binomial(r+s,r) gamma_(r+s)(x); refining blocks gives gamma_r(gamma_s(x))=((rs)!/(r!(s!)^r)) gamma_(rs)(x). Expanding blocks labelled by x or y gives the additive divided-power identity, and a degree-zero scalar supplies its nth power. The face-cancellation grouping gives d gamma_n(x)=gamma_(n-1)(x) dx. These calculations verify the standard positive-even shuffle divided-power construction underlying the stated unused claim. The precise presentation/conventions of bibliography item [6] are not independently read or attributed here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.3-homology-and-normal-complexes	2026-11-18 17:18:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The graded Leibniz rule makes cycles stable under multiplication and makes boundaries an ideal among cycles, so homology inherits the stated graded ring and module structures. Naturality of the shuffle map makes f-tilde a differential graded algebra map and its homology map a graded ring map. The homology identification with pi_n uses the already read simplicial abelian-group normalization. The restriction in (1.3.5) is N(A) tensor N(A)->N(A tensor A), followed by N of multiplication; hence NA is a differential graded subalgebra of A-tilde. The same restriction with M gives NA tensor NM->NM. No assertion about the still-unread next-page composite is made.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.4-evaluation-and-truncation	2026-11-18 17:26:32 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The fixed A in (3.1.4) is an ordinary Ring. Compose the shuffle chain map E-tilde tensor Homs_A(E,F)-tilde -> (E tensor_A Homs_A(E,F))-tilde with simplicial evaluation to F-tilde. Tensor-Hom adjunction, using the usual graded symmetry, gives (3.1.4.1). Its source is concentrated in nonpositive cohomological degrees. A chain map from such a complex to K sends degree zero to ker(d:K^0->K^1), so it factors through smart truncation t_(0]) K. This produces (3.1.4.2) without flatness or exactness assumptions.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.4-homotopy-Yoneda-and-shuffle	2026-11-18 17:26:32 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For any nonpositive complex Q, both chain maps and degree-minus-one chain homotopies Q->K factor through t_(0])K. Therefore Hot(Q,t_(0])K)=Hot(Q,K). The map (3.1.4.2) is a homotopy equivalence iff it induces isomorphisms on these Hot sets for every Q, by Yoneda in the homotopy category. Dold-Puppe normalization and the natural homotopy equivalence between unnormalized and normalized complexes reduce Q to the associated complex L-tilde of a simplicial A-Module L. The simplicial tensor-Hom adjunction, compatible with simplicial homotopies, identifies the source of the resulting map with Hot((L tensor E)-tilde,F-tilde). The chain tensor-Hom adjunction identifies its target with Hot(L-tilde tensor E-tilde,F-tilde). Under these identifications the map is precomposition with the shuffle L-tilde tensor E-tilde -> (L tensor E)-tilde. By the already read (1.2.2.1) this is a homotopy equivalence, so precomposition is an isomorphism. The locally rebound L is well typed throughout; no base A index need be inserted into the authorially abbreviated operators.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
