ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.5-module-cylinder-factorization	2026-11-18 17:29:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Let C(E)=A^(Delta1) tensor_A E, with endpoint inclusions j0,j1 and augmentation q:C(E)->E. Form F-prime=C(E) amalgamated_(j0,E,f) F in simplicial A-Modules. The other endpoint induces i:E->F-prime. In every degree the interval endpoints are distinct summands of the free A-module A^(Delta1), so the pushout relation on j0 does not meet j1(E); thus i is monic without a flatness hypothesis. The augmentation followed by f and Id_F induce p:F-prime->F with pi=f. The interval contraction fixing endpoint 0 extends A-linearly and descends through the pushout, giving an A-linear homotopy from Id_(F-prime) to the composite of p with the canonical section F->F-prime. Hence p is an A-homotopy equivalence.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.6-fibration-cokernel-criterion	2026-11-18 17:29:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Normalization is exact on simplicial abelian sheaves, with its inverse Dold-Puppe decomposition. If Nf is epimorphic in every positive degree, then N(coker f) vanishes there, so coker f is the constant simplicial abelian sheaf Coker pi_0(f). Conversely that cokernel identification forces Nf to be epimorphic in positive degrees. The induced A action on this constant simplicial quotient factors through pi_0(A), since the two faces of every 1-simplex act identically there. Thus the exact sequence is an exact sequence of simplicial A-Modules with the stated A->pi_0(A) action, including when f is originally an algebra map. The degree-zero map need not be epic.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.6.1-absolute-and-relative-path-factorization	2026-11-18 17:29:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For absolute A-Modules and A-Algebras, the underlying simplicial abelian sheaf is fibrant. Let P(F)=internal Homs(Delta1,F) and F-prime=E times_(f,F,d0) P(F); the map i takes e to its constant f(e)-path, and f-prime=d1 projection. All operations and endpoint maps are A-linear, and in the algebra case are A-algebra maps, because A acts through constant paths. The endpoint-pair map is a fibration, and E is fibrant, so f-prime is a fibration. Interval contraction gives i a homotopy inverse. In the second resp. case, use P_B(F) and fiber products over B; both E and F have structural fibrations to B by the proposition hypothesis, so the already checked relative argument (2.3.13.6) applies. The contraction and operations remain over B. The argument therefore proves the three intended cases after removing the duplicated category, without changing any triangle arrow.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.7-localization-and-homotopies	2026-11-18 17:32:23 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The constant-path map c:F->internal Homs(Delta1,F), and its relative analogue, is an A-homotopy equivalence by interval contraction. Therefore it is a quasi-isomorphism. Since d0 c=d1 c=Id, every functor inverting quasi-isomorphisms identifies d0 and d1 and hence every elementary A-homotopic pair. It consequently factors through the stated Hot category and its localization. Conversely localization of Hot inverts the original quasi-isomorphisms. The two universal properties identify the localized categories without a points hypothesis.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.7-constant-ring-normalization	2026-11-18 17:32:23 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	When A is the constant simplicial Ring associated to Lambda, simplicial A-Modules are simplicial objects of the abelian category of Lambda-Modules. The normalization equivalence and its inverse are Lambda-linear, identify simplicial homotopies with chain homotopies up to the already established normalization comparison, and identify pi_n with H_n. They therefore induce the stated equivalences of the raw, homotopy and quasi-isomorphism-localized categories C., Hot. and D. The three operators and their baseline dots are consistent. The proof of the subsequent fraction-calculus theorem is deliberately left open pending p63.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.8-pushout-cokernel-and-counterexample	2026-11-18 17:38:20 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	In the p62 square, s:X-prime->X is monic and Y is the pushout X amalg_(X-prime) Y-prime in simplicial A-Modules. Therefore t:Y-prime->Y is monic and the bottom u induces Coker(s)=X/X-prime isomorphic to Coker(t)=Y/Y-prime. The long exact homology sequence makes Coker(s) acyclic because s is a quasi-isomorphism, and then makes t a quasi-isomorphism. This is entirely an abelian-category calculation, requiring no points. The printed reversed quotient is not harmless notation: take constant A=Z, X-prime=Y-prime=0, and X=Y the nonzero contractible simplicial module corresponding to the two-term identity complex Z->Z. Then s and t are injective quasi-isomorphisms and the square is a pushout, but 0/X is not a subobject quotient. The correct quotient is X/0. The top u-prime has domain X-prime, so only the bottom u induces the displayed nontrivial cokernel map.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.8-trivial-fibration-pullbacks	2026-11-18 17:38:20 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For a fibrant quasi-isomorphism t of simplicial abelian sheaves, Nt is epic in positive degrees. Its H_0 map is epic as well. Locally, any y_0 differs from an image t(x_0) by a boundary d(y_1); lift y_1 through Nt_1, then add the boundary of that lift to x_0. This proves Nt_0 is epic. Dold-Puppe decomposition now makes t epic in every simplicial degree. Its kernel is acyclic by the long exact homology sequence. Pullback is again degreewise epic with the same kernel, hence is both fibrant and a quasi-isomorphism. Limits of A-Algebras, and of those over B, are created on the underlying simplicial abelian groups, so the same proof applies to all three cases without enough points.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.8-left-fraction-axioms	2026-11-18 17:38:20 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	In A-Mod, the cylinder factorization (3.1.5) replaces every denominator by a monic quasi-isomorphism up to an isomorphism in Hot. Pushout stability just proved gives the left Ore square. For left cancellation, use the p46 construction with the free interval tensor C_I(E)=Z^(I_n) tensor_Z E, padding a zero-length homotopy by a constant segment. Given a homotopy on the submodule X-prime, form M=C_I(X-prime) amalg_(X-prime direct-sum X-prime) (X direct-sum X). Its map to C_I(X) is monic degreewise, since endpoint summands and the interior interval basis give the stated intersection. The map C_I(X-prime)->M is a pushout of two copies of the monic quasi-isomorphism s, hence is a quasi-isomorphism. The map C_I(s) is a quasi-isomorphism by the natural interval-contraction equivalences C_I(E)->E. Two-out-of-three makes M->C_I(X) a monic quasi-isomorphism. Pushing it out along the given homotopy produces a quasi-isomorphism out of the target and a homotopy equalizing the two maps. Every construction is A-linear. This verifies left cancellation without a points argument.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.8-right-fraction-axioms	2026-11-18 17:38:20 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The path factorization (3.1.6.1) replaces denominators by fibrant quasi-isomorphisms up to homotopy equivalence. Their pullback stability gives right Ore squares. For cancellation, repeat the already inspected p52 evaluation construction within A-Modules or A-Algebras: P=Homs(I_n,Y), Q=(Y times Y) times_(Y-prime times Y-prime) Homs(I_n,Y-prime). The map P->Q is a fibration by the endpoint evaluation result; Q->Homs(I_n,Y-prime) is a pullback of t times t and is a quasi-isomorphism by the kernel calculation. Constant paths give natural homotopy equivalences, so Homs(I_n,t) is a quasi-isomorphism. Two-out-of-three makes P->Q a fibrant quasi-isomorphism. The given homotopy supplies X->Q; its pullback with P is the required denominator on X and lifts the homotopy. Relative products/path objects give the same proof in Hotfib(A-Alg/B), retaining fibrancy over B. Thus both fraction directions hold for modules and the stated right fractions hold for the two algebra categories.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.1.8-relative-localization-comparison	2026-11-18 17:38:20 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For any A-Algebra E->B, apply the absolute A-algebra path factorization to obtain a functorial map E->R_B(E) which is an absolute A-homotopy equivalence, hence a quasi-isomorphism over B, and whose target is fibrant over B. This map need not have a homotopy inverse over B; only its quasi-isomorphism property is used. Naturality and two-out-of-three make R_B preserve quasi-isomorphisms. The natural maps give inverse equivalences after localization of the raw category and its fibrant-over-B subcategory. Relative constant paths show that localization of this subcategory agrees with localization of Hotfib, by the universal-property argument already checked on p55. This establishes the final assertion of (ii)-prime without using enough points or assuming relative invertibility of the initial replacement map.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1-degreewise-split-sequence	2026-11-18 17:38:20 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	In degree n, Z^(Delta1) is free on the n+2 monotone maps [n]->[1]. The two endpoint maps d^0,d^1 pick distinct constant maps, giving independent basis elements. Quotienting by the first yields gamma; quotienting by both yields sigma. The remaining endpoint embeds a copy of Z into gamma by the printed Z^(d^1), and its quotient is sigma. Choosing the nonconstant basis elements supplies a section in every degree, proving the asserted degreewise split exact sequence. This section is not asserted simplicial: faces can turn a nonconstant simplex into an endpoint. The gamma ranks are n+1 and sigma ranks n, consistent with the printed definitions. The continuing reduced-chain description remains open at p64.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1-sphere-and-normalized-generators	2026-11-18 17:46:30 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The coequalizer of the two endpoint maps Delta0 to Delta1 identifies the endpoints and yields the pointed simplicial circle. Its reduced free abelian group is sigma. Write v_0=d^1, v_1=d^0 and e for the nondegenerate edge. In gamma the class v_1 vanishes. With normalization N_1=ker(d_1), choose g=s_0(v_0)-e: d_1(g)=0 and d_0(g)=v_0. Thus the displayed differential is Id in bases (g,v_0), not a sign error. In sigma choose -e for the degree-one generator; the quotient maps g to -e and kills degree zero. Normalized free chains have no terms above degree one here, and exactness of normalization gives the displayed evident short exact sequence of complexes. The printed external [10] reference has not been separately inspected.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1-constant-sheaf-and-tensor-exactness	2026-11-18 17:46:30 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The gamma and sigma initially defined in simplicial abelian groups are viewed degreewise as constant sheaves on T. They are not constant in simplicial degree: their ranks are n+1 and n. Hence the capitalized Module convention makes the printed constant wording meaningful. The sequence Z->gamma->sigma is split as abelian groups in every degree by the distinct endpoint basis calculation on p63. Passing to constant sheaves and tensoring with E therefore preserves its exactness without a flatness assumption. A acts on the E factor; i_E and both quotient maps are A-linear and natural in E.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1-cone-contraction	2026-11-18 17:46:30 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The order-preserving map max:Delta1 times Delta1->Delta1 contracts the interval toward vertex v_1 while fixing v_1. Linearization and quotient by that vertex give a simplicial homotopy between Id_gamma and zero. Tensor this homotopy with E, with A acting on E, to obtain an A-linear contraction of gamma E. The argument does not require E to be flat and does not mistake a degreewise splitting for a simplicial splitting.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1-path-square-and-contraction	2026-11-18 17:46:30 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The left square defines phi F as ker(d_0:Homs(Delta1,F)->F); the right defines omega F as ker(d_0,d_1). All eight arrows have the displayed domains and targets. A acts through constant paths in A. Precomposition by max(t,s) contracts every path whose v_1 endpoint is zero to the zero path, stays in ker d_0, and is A-linear for this constant-path action. Thus phi F is homotopically trivial as stated. No contractibility is asserted for omega F; exactness of the next-page sequence remains to be checked on that page.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.7-path-sequence-exactness	2026-11-18 17:49:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The kernel of p_F=d_1 restricted to ker d_0 is precisely omega F. Endpoint evaluation with both endpoints is a fibration by the earlier path-lifting argument; pullback along {0} times F makes p_F a fibration, with the d_0/d_1 roles interchanged as stated. By the previously checked fibration criterion its cokernel is constant and equals Coker(pi_0 p_F). The contraction of phi F checked on p64 gives pi_0(phi F)=0, so this cokernel is pi_0 F. Equivalently, in degree zero the image consists of boundaries of one-simplices with d_0=0. This verifies all terms and exactness in (3.2.1.7), including the final constant simplicial object and its induced A-module structure.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.8-tensor-hom-adjunction	2026-11-18 17:49:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Apply the A-linear tensor-Hom adjunction to gamma tensor_Z E and sigma tensor_Z E. Internal Z-linear Hom from the free simplicial module on Delta1 is the internal path object. Vanishing on the first endpoint submodule identifies Hom from gamma with phi F; vanishing on both identifies Hom from sigma with omega F. Currying therefore gives both displayed ordinary Hom isomorphisms and their internal simplicial and global simplicial counterparts. The omitted A subscripts follow the explicit convention already checked on p60. This derivation confirms the direction of the French explanatory sentence and exposes its reversal in the supplied English.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.9-diagram-types-and-footnote	2026-11-18 17:49:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The first diagram compares the cone sequence for E with the path sequence for sigma E. Since sigma_0=0, (sigma E)_0=0 and hence pi_0(sigma E)=0, justifying its short bottom row and the printed note. Its first vertical map has the unit type E->omega sigma E and the third is Id_sigma E. The second compares the cone sequence of omega F with the path sequence of F; its third vertical map is the counit sigma omega F->F and its first is Id_omega F. These type and exactness checks account for all eleven plus twelve displayed arrows. Existence and commutativity of the middle maps remain to be verified with the proof continuing on p66; no assertion of a completed lemma proof is made here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.9-h1-and-h2	2026-11-18 17:53:17 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Take h(s,t)=max(s,t) on the ordered interval. At vertex d^0=1 it is the constant-one map; at d^1=0 it is Id. On a path q, h_1([s] tensor q) is the path t |-> q(max(s,t)), extended Z-linearly. This is A-linear for the constant-path A action. On phi F, q(1)=0, so the s=1 endpoint vanishes; the map factors through gamma phi F and lands in phi F because max(s,1)=1. Its s=0 endpoint is Id, giving h_2 d^1=Id. The printed d_0^0 in the first h_1 identity is the indexed [0]->[1] coface, hence is retained rather than corrected as a defect.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.9-second-exact-sequence-diagram	2026-11-18 17:53:17 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Restrict h_2 along gamma(omega F)->gamma(phi F). On the included omega F endpoint it is the usual inclusion into phi F, so the first square of diagram (ii) commutes. Its composite with p_F evaluates q(max(s,0))=q(s). Because a loop vanishes at both endpoints, this evaluation factors through sigma omega F and is exactly the sigma/omega counit. The second square therefore commutes with the stated adjunction arrow. The two auxiliary squares on p66 express the same naturality/evaluation calculation. The first vertical arrows are inclusions; gamma preserves their monicity degreewise because gamma_n is free abelian. The second auxiliary square legitimately uses ordinary arrows as printed.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.9-evaluation-and-h3-types	2026-11-18 17:53:17 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The composite d_1 h_1 sends [s] tensor q to q(s), the tensor/path adjunction evaluation. Equivalently, the corresponding endpoint composite of Homs(h,F) is Id because max(s,0)=s. This proves assertion b) and closes the counit verification for diagram (ii), without consulting external item [11]. The erroneous functor placeholder -1 is not a variable and is correctly repaired to -. The map h_3 can be typed as [x] tensor e |-> (t |-> [max(t,x)] tensor e), with Delta1 tensor E the simplicial-set copower, canonically the free-module tensor. Its further reduction and the unit diagram (i) remain open until the following page.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.9-unit-diagram-completion	2026-11-18 17:58:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and counterexample check	The h_3 formula from p66 sends [x] tensor e to the path t |-> [max(t,x)] tensor e. Quotient the target interval at vertex 1. Since max(1,x)=1 and max(t,1)=1, this factors through h_4:gamma E->phi gamma E. Evaluation at t=0 is Id_gamma E. Compose h_4 with phi of the quotient gamma E->sigma E to obtain the middle map in diagram (i). On i_E(e)=[0] tensor e this is the loop t |-> [t] tensor e in sigma E, which is the sigma/omega unit by tensor-Hom adjunction. Its other endpoint is zero. The two squares of diagram (i) therefore commute, with identity on sigma E. Together with the p66 counit calculation this completes the proof of the lemma.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.10-unit-and-counit-quasi-isomorphisms	2026-11-18 17:58:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and counterexample check	The middle terms in both lemma diagrams are contractible. Naturality of the connecting homomorphisms in their exact sequences gives the unit E->omega sigma E as an isomorphism on every homotopy sheaf. For the counit, the same argument gives the positive-degree isomorphisms when pi_0(F)=0, and pi_0 of both source and target then vanishes. Conversely sigma omega F has degree zero zero, so a quasi-isomorphism from it to F forces pi_0(F)=0. This checks both directions of the stated if-and-only-if, not just sufficiency.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.11-descent-and-full-faithfulness	2026-11-18 17:58:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and counterexample check	Tensoring with sigma preserves A-linear simplicial homotopies. Applying the path construction to a homotopy and restricting its endpoint kernels preserves homotopies for omega as well. The cone and path exact sequences give natural shifts pi_n(sigma E)=pi_(n-1)(E) for n>=1 with pi_0(sigma E)=0, and pi_n(omega F)=pi_(n+1)(F) for n>=0; hence both preserve quasi-isomorphisms. The adjunction, unit, counit and triangle identities descend to Hot and then localization D. Since the unit is invertible in D, sigma is fully faithful, so each permitted suspension transition in the Ext definition is an isomorphism.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.12-and-suspension-signs	2026-11-18 17:58:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and counterexample check	For n nonnegative with n>=-p, the corrected reading Hom_D(sigma^n E,sigma^(n+p) F) is well-typed. Reindexing m=n+i gives Ext^p(sigma^i E,F)=Ext^(p-i)(E,F); shifting the target gives Ext^p(E,sigma^i F)=Ext^(p+i)(E,F). These agree with (13) and (14) and with the visible lower vertical trace of the faded plus. A minus exponent would contradict both identities and the printed bound. The mathematical determination is firm, while the diplomatic pixel reading is explicitly medium confidence pending second inspection.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.13-counterexample-and-repair	2026-11-18 17:58:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and counterexample check	Take the point topos, constant A=Z, and E=F=Z as constant simplicial modules. Every path into the discrete constant module is constant, so phi F=omega F=0. By full faithfulness of sigma, Ext^0_Z(Z,Z)=Hom_D(Z,Z)=Z. For p=i=1, the printed final comparison in (13) would identify this Z with Ext^1_Z(Z,omega Z)=Ext^1_Z(Z,0)=0. This contradiction is independent of the ambiguity in the sign of the Ext exponent, since the middle group has degree zero and the right target is zero. A sufficient repair is pi_j(F)=0 for 0<=j<i. Successive loop shifts and the connected-case counit then show sigma^i omega^i F->F is a quasi-isomorphism. Formula (14), with exponent p-i, gives Ext^p(E,omega^i F)=Ext^(p-i)(E,sigma^i omega^i F)=Ext^(p-i)(E,F). Only this last comparison receives the added hypothesis; the two suspension identities remain unconditional.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1-constant-ring-translation	2026-11-18 17:58:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and counterexample check	For a constant coefficient Ring Lambda, normalization and the already checked shuffle comparison identify N(sigma tensor E), up to natural chain homotopy, with N(sigma) tensor N(E). Since N(sigma) is Z concentrated in homological degree one, this is the usual translation E[1] in the cohomological nonpositive convention. Thus the stated suspension/translation identification holds in Hot and D. The sentence about Ext continues on the next page and is not certified beyond the inspected boundary.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.15-hyperext-identification	2026-11-18 18:06:19 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For a constant Ring A, normalization identifies simplicial A-Modules with nonpositive cochain complexes. The normalized/unnormalized comparison is a natural chain homotopy equivalence. The suspension calculation completed on p67 identifies sigma with translation [1]. For every integer p, choose n>=max(0,-p); both translated complexes are nonpositive. The embedding of their localized bounded-above category into the derived category is fully faithful. Thus the stabilized Hom groups defining Ext^p agree with hyperext of NE,NF and of the unnormalized complexes, as asserted in (15).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.16-free-module-localization	2026-11-18 18:06:19 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	At each point, Whitehead implies that a weak homotopy equivalence of simplicial sets induces a quasi-isomorphism on free integral chains. Its mapping cone is a bounded-below acyclic complex of free abelian groups, hence contractible by successive splittings. Tensoring with the stalk of A preserves that contraction; no flatness of A is required. Enough points therefore detects the resulting quasi-isomorphism of A-Module sheaves. The forgetful functor also preserves weak equivalences. The free/forgetful unit, counit and triangle identities descend through localization and prove the displayed adjunction. For p>=0, full faithfulness of sigma identifies Ext^p with Hom_D(E,sigma^p F), giving (16). The right side uses only the underlying simplicial Z-Module F because sigma is the fixed integral suspension tensor.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.16-right-fractions	2026-11-18 18:06:19 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Simplicial abelian sheaves are Kan, including sigma^p F. When X is Kan, the previously verified right-fraction description applies. A denominator Xprime->X with Xprime Kan factors as a trivial cofibration followed by a fibration; the former is invertible in the homotopy category of Kan objects, so the fibrant denominator represents the same object up to isomorphism in the denominator category. This proves the asserted cofinality at the homotopy-category level, without assuming a strict section over X.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.17-mixed-Hom-types-and-totalization	2026-11-18 18:06:19 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Addition in M makes Hom_T(Y_j,M_i) an abelian group. Contravariance in Y gives the upper cosimplicial index j, while covariance in M gives the lower simplicial index i. The free A-Module adjunction identifies these terms with Hom_A(A^(Y_j),M_i). For the ordinary Hom complex in cohomological degree n=j-i, the diagonal is a PRODUCT over j,i>=0 with j-i=n; its differential is d_M f-(-1)^n f d_Y. It can be related to the commuting-bicomplex convention by componentwise signs. The direct-sum totalization printed on p6 must not be silently substituted on these infinite mixed diagonals: even for Y a constant point and M constant Z, the degree-zero identity family in all unnormalized degrees belongs to the product but not the direct sum. Page 68 names the Hom complex without displaying an integral symbol; its precise mixed totalization convention and the cohomology claim are left open to the continuing p69 argument. No original-source correction is inferred solely from an unstated convention.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.18-product-totalization	2026-11-18 18:10:03 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Use the ordinary product Hom complex from the p68 type check. Its degree-p cocycles are chain maps C->D[p], and coboundaries are chain homotopies. Thus H^p Hom(C,D)=H^0 Hom(C,D[p]). The natural chain homotopy equivalence D[p]~(sigma^p M)-tilde from (3.2.1.11) induces a homotopy equivalence on Hom(C,-); no projectivity of C is needed for this step. The previously checked normalization and chain/simplicial homotopy comparison identifies the next H^0 with pi_0 Homs_A(A^(Y),sigma^p M). The free/forgetful adjunction is compatible with interval tensors, identifying this with pi_0 Homs(Y,sigma^p M). The latter is Hot(Y,sigma^p M) by (2.3.2.1), since the target is a simplicial abelian sheaf and hence Kan. All four intermediate comparisons and (18) are justified under the product convention. The incompatible direct-sum interpretation is not certified; I-Q005 preserves this specific convention issue for second review.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.19-hypercovering-limit	2026-11-18 18:10:03 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Apply the preceding comparison naturally to every fibrant denominator Xprime->X in the cofinal system established on p68. Pullback along denominator maps acts contravariantly on the first Hom variable, and the comparison respects these maps and homotopies. Taking the indicated filtered inductive limit and using (16) gives the displayed Ext formula for p>=0. Since X is Kan, a fibration over X has Kan source. Thus the theorem does not need to repeat the Kan condition on Xprime. The symbol Lambda denotes the ordinary Ring viewed as constant simplicial A, consistent with the standing hypothesis. Enough points remains in force.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.1.20-hypercoverings-and-Cech	2026-11-18 18:10:03 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	For X the constant final object, a fibrant quasi-isomorphism Xprime->X is locally an acyclic Kan complex. Vanishing homotopy groups gives local fillers for every boundary, equivalently epimorphic matching maps Xprime_n->Hom(partial Delta(n),Xprime), including the degree-zero cover of the final object. Conversely these local boundary fillers give horn fillers and vanishing homotopy sheaves, hence a fibrant quasi-isomorphism. This verifies the hypercovering characterization independently of consulting the cited SGA appendix. The additional semi-representability restriction is preserved as an attributed bibliographic statement, not independently certified here. When F is constant, normalization in its simplicial direction leaves only degree zero; the mixed Hom cohomology reduces to the usual alternating coface cochain complex Hom(Xprime_n,F), giving Cech cohomology of the hypercovering. Also Hom_Lambda(Lambda,F)=Gamma(T,F), so Ext^p_Lambda(Lambda,F) is ordinary sheaf cohomology. This explains the claimed specialization/generalization under the established totalization convention.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.0-cone-pushout-and-signs	2026-11-18 18:12:17 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	In the abelian category of simplicial A-Modules, the pushout of E->gamma E and u:E->F is coker((-i_E,u):E->gamma E direct-sum F). Since i_E is a monomorphism, this column map is monic, proving exactness of (0). A commutative square of arrows induces a map of these cokernels, proving functoriality. For F=0 the quotient is sigma E. For u=Id_E the map (x,e)|->x+i_E(e) identifies the cokernel with gamma E, so the stated sign convention gives the claimed special case.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.1-cone-arrows	2026-11-18 18:12:17 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The pushout map j:F->C(u) is monic because it is the pushout of the monomorphism i_E. Quotienting C(u) by j(F) gives gamma E/i_E(E)=sigma E, so p is epimorphic and is precisely the map induced by F->0. The three arrows in (1) have the indicated types. The page calls this a sequence of arrows, not a short exact sequence beginning with E; no unjustified exactness at E or F is inferred.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.2-fiber-pullback-and-cokernel	2026-11-18 18:12:17 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The pullback F(u)=E times_F phi F consists of pairs (e,q) with u(e)=p_F(q), hence is the kernel of the row (u,-p_F). The earlier path exact sequence gives coker(p_F)=pi_0(F), viewed as a constant simplicial module. Thus coker(u,-p_F)=pi_0(F)/image(E), and E->pi_0(F) factors through pi_0(E). Its cokernel is exactly Coker pi_0(u), with its pi_0(A)-action pulled back along A->pi_0(A). This verifies (2) without requiring u itself to be fibrant or surjective. The printed parenthetical justification is left open until the next page is read.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.2-parenthetical-completion	2026-11-18 18:14:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, type and counterexample check	For simplicial modules, a map is fibrant when its normalized maps are epimorphic in positive degrees. Since p_F is fibrant, the summand -p_F makes (u,-p_F):E direct-sum phi F->F fibrant irrespective of u. The path module phi F is contractible, so pi_0(E direct-sum phi F)=pi_0(E) and the induced map is exactly pi_0(u). The cited fibration/cokernel criterion therefore proves the two right-hand exactness assertions on p70. This completes the parenthesis without adding a hypothesis on u.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.3-functoriality-and-inducing-square	2026-11-18 18:14:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, type and counterexample check	Functoriality of the pullback gives F(u) naturally from a commutative square of arrows. For E=0 it is ker(p_F)=omega F, and for u=Id_F projection to phi F is an isomorphism. For the natural inclusion i, the required square has top arrow 0->F, bottom arrow u:E->F, left vertical arrow 0->E and right vertical arrow Id_F. Applying the fiber construction gives omega F->F(u), explicitly q|->(0,q). This is also the kernel of q:F(u)->E and is monic. The phrase saying i is induced by the injection 0->F has the wrong source-component target: it must say 0->E. The original F is unequivocal in the 800-dpi crop. Corrected French and English receive this single-letter repair; diplomatic French retains it.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.4-triangle-morphism-types	2026-11-18 18:14:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, type and counterexample check	Write a triangle as (L->M->N->sigma L). A morphism consists of f:L->Lprime, g:M->Mprime and h:N->Nprime; the fourth vertical map is forced to be sigma(f), not independent data. The three commuting squares have matching domains/codomains, and componentwise composition preserves commutativity because sigma is a functor. Isomorphisms are exactly triples of isomorphisms, which makes the definition of distinguished triangle by isomorphism to a cone triangle well-formed. The image under normalization is an ordinary derived-category triangle since N sigma is naturally isomorphic to translation [1]. This page does not yet assert the following page distinction theorem.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.4-pseudo-triangle-footnote	2026-11-18 18:14:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, type and counterexample check	The fiber sequence omega F->F(u)->E->F cannot generally be identified with an ordinary derived triangle: sigma omega F->F need not be invertible. For the point topos with constant A=Z, take E=0 and F=Z constant. Then omega F=0 and F(u)=0, so the four-term fiber sequence is 0->0->0->Z. Its last object is not the suspension of its first object, and it cannot be a distinguished triangle in D(Z). This directly verifies the footnote claim that the proposed pseudo-triangle notion does not coincide with the usual distinguished notion. The author opinion about interest and the attribution of terminology to Quillen are preserved as source statements, not independently bibliographically verified.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.5-cone-signs	2026-11-18 18:21:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign and type check	For C(v)^i=L^(i+1) direct-sum M^i the displayed matrix means d(a,m)=(-d_L a,v a+d_M m). Squaring gives (0,-v d_L a+d_M v a)=0 because v is a chain map. The normalization N gamma from p64 has Z in degrees -1 and 0 with differential +Id. The tensor differential on N gamma tensor L therefore gives (-d a,a+d b), exactly the differential on C(Id_L). Thus the printed signs and cone identity are compatible with the previously checked convention.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.5-amalgamated-sum-exactness	2026-11-18 18:21:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign and type check	The first map is j(x)=(0,-x,vx) and the second q(a,b,c)=(a,vb+c). Both commute with the differentials; qj=0. The second map is degreewise surjective since (a,m) lifts to (a,0,m). Its kernel consists of a=0 and c=-vb, hence (0,b,-vb)=j(-b); j is monic. This proves the exact sequence (*) and identifies its quotient with the amalgamated sum. The contracting homotopy of C(Id_L) is h(a,b)=(b,0), and dh+hd=Id.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.5-normalization-square	2026-11-18 18:21:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign and type check	The inclusion i_E sends E to the constant vertex v_0 in gamma tensor E. Under normalized Alexander-Whitney, all summands involving positive normalized degree of the constant vertex vanish; the surviving summand is v_0 tensor NE. In C(Id_NE) this is the canonical inclusion x|->(0,x). Consequently alpha composed with Ni_E is precisely that inclusion, proving the printed square commutes. This is a direct check with the established Alexander-Whitney formula, without independently verifying the external citation [10] 5.32.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.5-exact-sequence-grid	2026-11-18 18:21:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign and type check	Exactness of normalization sends the defining cone sequence to the upper short exact sequence. The map alpha direct-sum Id sends (-Ni_E x,Nu x) to (0,-x,Nu x), the first map of (*) for v=Nu. The left square therefore commutes and the quotient supplies the unique right vertical map NC(u)->C(Nu). All eleven arrows have matching domains and codomains. The proof sentence concerning alpha direct-sum Id continues on p73 and is not treated as fully read here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.5-comparison-quasi-isomorphism	2026-11-18 18:24:05 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Complete the p72 argument: Alexander-Whitney is a chain homotopy equivalence and hence so is alpha direct-sum Id. In the map of short exact sequences, the left arrow is Id and the middle arrow is a quasi-isomorphism. The associated cohomology long exact sequences therefore imply that NC(u)->C(Nu) is a quasi-isomorphism. The right square is obtained functorially from the map of arrows (E->F)->(E->0). Naturality of Alexander-Whitney identifies its right vertical map with N sigma E->N sigma tensor NE=(NE)[1]. All four square arrows are compatible with the quotient maps.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.5-reflection-of-distinguished-triangles	2026-11-18 18:24:05 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	The resulting four-object comparison has Id on NE and NF, the cone quasi-isomorphism on the third object, and the natural suspension comparison on the fourth. It identifies NX with the ordinary cone triangle, proving preservation. For reflection, represent the first morphism of NX by a roof NE<-P->NF in nonpositive complexes; the nonpositive representative can be obtained by good truncation. Dold-Puppe realizes P and the chain map by a simplicial module and an actual simplicial map. Its cone triangle Y is distinguished, and the ordinary uniqueness of a cone triangle up to isomorphism gives NX isomorphic to NY. Full faithfulness of the localized Dold-Puppe equivalence, together with the natural suspension comparison, lifts that isomorphism to triangles X and Y. This supplies the implicit justification in the concise printed converse without inserting new hypotheses.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.6-restriction-and-homotopy-indices	2026-11-18 18:24:05 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and type check	Restriction of scalars preserves the underlying simplicial modules, the tensor with gamma and sigma over Z, and the cokernel defining C(u). It also preserves weak equivalences, so it carries cone triangles and their isomorphisms to distinguished triangles. After restriction to Z, normalization gives the ordinary cohomology exact sequence. With pi_n=H^(-n), the connecting map has target pi_(n-1)(E); connectivity gives the terminal pi_0(G)->0. The connecting maps respect the pi_0(A) action: locally lift a scalar to A_0 and use naturality after restriction along the constant polynomial-ring map Z[t]->A sending t to that zero simplex; the action descends to pi_0(A). This checks the displayed module types and indices. The prose assertion after the final comma continues on p74.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.6-boundary-completion	2026-11-18 18:27:31 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign-convention and type check	The p73 long exact sequence is completed by the canonical isomorphism pi_n(sigma E)=pi_(n-1)(E), obtained from the normalized suspension comparison. The connecting map is the composite pi_n(G)->pi_n(sigma E)->pi_(n-1)(E). This completes the exactness assertion and agrees with the indices and scalar-linearity verification recorded on p73.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.7-positive-column-and-quotient	2026-11-18 18:27:31 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign-convention and type check	Let r:gamma F direct-sum C(u)->C(j) be the standard quotient whose kernel is (-i_F,j). The printed upper row instead has the positive column (i_F,j). It is still exact with the unlabeled quotient rprime(a,c)=r(-a,c): its kernel is exactly the image of (i_F,j). The second-summand inclusion, hence k:C(u)->C(j), is unchanged. Projection (0,Id) sends that positive column to j, so the left square commutes with the unlabeled vertical Id_F. Thus this printed sign does not force an original error; the quotient sign convention is explicitly accounted for rather than silently changing i_F.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.7-lambda-and-first-square	2026-11-18 18:27:31 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign-convention and type check	The induced map lambda sends r(a,c) to p(c); this is well-defined since pj=0. It is functorial and lambda k=p, which proves the first displayed square commutes. Projection gamma F direct-sum C(u)->C(u) has contractible kernel gamma F, and the left vertical map is Id_F. The cohomology long exact sequence comparison therefore makes lambda a quasi-isomorphism. Equivalently, the kernel of lambda identifies with gamma F by writing any c in ker p locally as jf and using the cone relation. The printed diagram is well-typed for arbitrary simplicial A.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.7-reduction-to-identity	2026-11-18 18:27:31 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof, sign-convention and type check	The arrow-category map (u:E->F)->(Id_F:F->F) with components (u,Id_F) induces a map t:C(j_u)->C(j_Id). Naturality gives q_Id t=q_u and lambda_Id t=(sigma u)lambda_u. A homotopy q_Id equivalent to -lambda_Id therefore pulls back to q_u equivalent to -(sigma u)lambda_u, justifying the printed reduction to u=Id_F. Under C(Id_F)=gamma F, j identifies with i_F. The construction of the required identity-case homotopy continues on p75 and is not certified from the unread continuation.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.7-identity-case-explicit-homotopy	2026-11-18 18:30:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted explicit homotopy, proof and type check	For the punctual topos and F=Z, normalize sequence (**). In degree -1 one obtains Z direct-sum Z, and in degree 0 the quotient of Z direct-sum Z by (-x,x), identified with Z by (a,b)|->a+b. Thus the differential is (Id,Id). The two maps to N sigma=Z in degree -1 are the two projections. Their sum is null-homotopic: take h:C^0=Z->(N sigma)^(-1)=Z to be Id and all other h components zero. Then dh+hd equals pr_1+pr_2 in degree -1 and is zero otherwise. Dold-Puppe carries this to a simplicial homotopy. Tensoring that homotopy with F gives an A-linear homotopy for general F; no flatness is required, and the underlying integer sequence is degreewise split. Together with the p74 naturality reduction this proves the second square and its negative sign.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.8-rotation-and-missing-sigma	2026-11-18 18:30:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted explicit homotopy, proof and type check	For a cone triangle E->F->C(u)->sigma E, the cone triangle of j:F->C(u) is F->C(u)->C(j)->sigma F. The quasi-isomorphism lambda:C(j)->sigma E commutes with the third map strictly and with the last map up to homotopy, where that last map on the rotated triangle is -sigma u. In the derived category this gives an isomorphism to the rotated triangle. Transport along an isomorphism of the original distinguished triangle gives the assertion for f,g,h. The final object must be sigma F both because -sigma f:sigma E->sigma F and because the fourth object suspends the first. The original F is therefore a genuine missing-symbol error; it is retained only diplomatically. The hypothesis reference .6 remains valid as the arbitrary-ring/general-triangle setting and is not forced to be .7 merely because .7 proves rotation.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.9-null-homotopy-and-factorizations	2026-11-18 18:30:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted explicit homotopy, proof and type check	A null-homotopy of gf:E->G is equivalent to a map H:gamma E->G with H i_E=gf, since gamma is the cylinder with one endpoint collapsed. The pushout definition of C(f) then gives a map C(f)->G restricting to g, and conversely such a map supplies H. By the adjunction gamma left-adjoint phi, H corresponds to K:E->phi G with p_G K=gf. The pair (f,K) is precisely a lift E->F(g)=F times_G phi G, proving equivalence with the third condition. All factorizations and homotopies are A-linear. This expands the printed proof without changing its wording.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.10-variance-and-boundary-index	2026-11-18 18:30:49 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted explicit homotopy, proof and type check	The displayed sequence is contravariant in the first variable: the arrows E->F->G induce Ext^i(G,M)->Ext^i(F,M)->Ext^i(E,M). Precomposition with the triangle boundary G->sigma E and the suspension identification shifts the connecting group to Ext^(i+1)(G,M). The displayed variance and indices are consistent. The proposition statement and proof continue on p76, so no claim is made here to have read their remaining hypotheses or proof.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.10-left-fractions-and-cone	2026-11-18 18:39:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and diagram type check	For a cone triangle E->F->C(u), represent a derived map F->M by F->Mprime<-M with the second map a quasi-isomorphism. If its composite with u is zero in the derived category, the left-fraction calculus supplies a further quasi-isomorphism out of Mprime making that composite zero in the homotopy category. Lemma 3.2.2.9 then factors the resulting map through C(u). Conversely the composite E->F->C(u) is null-homotopic, proving image equals kernel in Hom(C(u),M)->Hom(F,M)->Hom(E,M).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.10-fiber-comparison-and-right-fractions	2026-11-18 18:39:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and diagram type check	Replace E->F->C(f) by the short exact sequence with injection (-i_E,f):E->gamma E direct-sum F, using the contractibility of gamma E. Renaming the middle object F gives 0->E->F->G->0. In the printed grid the lower map (g,-p_G) is epic because g is epic, and its kernel is F(g). The middle vertical (Id,0) is a homotopy equivalence because phi G is contractible; the right vertical is Id, so the homology long exact sequences imply E->F(g) is a quasi-isomorphism. Replacing the lower middle object by F uses its projection in the homotopy category: g pr_F differs from (g,-p_G) by p_G pr_phi, which factors through the contractible phi G and is null-homotopic; strict commutativity of that replacement is not asserted. For M->F whose composite with g vanishes in the derived category, right fractions permit a further quasi-isomorphism into a representative source making the composite null-homotopic. Lemma 3.2.2.9 lifts it to F(g), and E->F(g) is invertible in the derived category. This proves the second Hom exactness statement.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.2.10-extension-to-long-exact-sequences	2026-11-18 18:39:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted proof and diagram type check	Apply the two Hom exactness statements to successive rotated distinguished triangles and use the colimit definition of Ext together with the suspension identifications to obtain both displayed long exact sequences. Filtered colimits of abelian groups preserve exactness. This uses the valid sigma suspension comparisons of 3.2.1.13 and 3.2.1.14 and does not require the unrestricted omega comparison qualified on p67. The displayed covariant boundary advances Ext^i(M,G) to Ext^(i+1)(M,E), consistently with the triangle boundary G->sigma E.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.1-exact-grid-and-quasi-isomorphism	2026-11-18 18:43:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted exactness, sign and functoriality proof check	Write C(u)=(gamma E direct-sum F)/im(-i_E,u). The upper square commutes because pr_1(-i_E,u)=-i_E; the lower left square commutes because pr_2(-i_E,u)=u. The quotient maps induce p:C(u)->sigma E and s:C(u)->G, the latter from v pr_2. Since u is monic, gamma E->C(u), a|->[(a,0)], is monic. If v f=0, locally f=u e and [(a,u e)]=[(a+i_E e,0)], so its image is ker s. Thus 0->gamma E->C(u)->G->0 is exact. Contractibility of gamma E makes s a quasi-isomorphism. All four internal squares and all eighteen arrow incidences are consistent.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.2-distinguished-triangle-and-representability	2026-11-18 18:43:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted exactness, sign and functoriality proof check	The cone triangle transports along s:C(u)->G to the displayed triangle with boundary p s^{-1}; its functoriality follows from functoriality of gamma and the quotient. Conversely, for any arrow u, take the short exact sequence E->gamma E direct-sum F->C(u) with first map (-i_E,u). Let Cprime be the cone of that injection. Its canonical comparison s_prime:Cprime->C(u) is a quasi-isomorphism. In representatives (a,(b,f)), its suspension map is [a], whereas p s_prime is [b]. Their difference [b-a] factors through gamma E, because b-a annihilates the defining relation (-i_E e,(-i_E e,u e)); hence the difference is null-homotopic. Projection of the middle object to F and identity on C(u) therefore give an isomorphism of the associated triangle with the original cone triangle in the derived category. This proves the every-distinguished-triangle assertion with the printed signs.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.3-functoriality-and-baer-addition	2026-11-18 18:43:47 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted exactness, sign and functoriality proof check	The pushout map of short exact sequences has endpoint maps f and Id_G, so the boundary is (sigma f) chi(X); the pullback map has endpoint maps Id_E and g, so its boundary is chi(X) g. Cones and suspension commute with finite direct sums. A Baer sum is formed by taking X direct-sum Y, pulling back by the diagonal of G, then pushing out by addition on E. The two displayed naturality identities therefore give chi(X+Y)=chi(X)+chi(Y); the split extension gives zero. This establishes that the map from Yoneda Ext^1 in the abelian category A-Mod to derived Ext^1 is a functorial homomorphism. No injectivity or surjectivity is claimed yet on this page.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.5-exactness-and-adjunction-types	2026-11-18 18:45:55 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted exactness, adjunction type and sign proof check	The cone sequence c_M is exact for every M. The fiber sequence f_M ends in M->0 precisely under the stated pi_0(M)=0 condition, since the image of p_M is the kernel of M->pi_0(M). The adjunction counit alpha has source sigma omega and target Id, while the unit beta goes from Id to omega sigma. These agree with the earlier constructions.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.6-negative-extension-classes	2026-11-18 18:45:55 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted exactness, adjunction type and sign proof check	For c_M the maps p,s:C(i_M)->sigma M are induced by the first and second projections of gamma M direct-sum gamma M. Their sum is null-homotopic by the explicit normalized homotopy checked on p75. Thus p=-s in the derived category and chi(c_M)=p s^{-1}=-Id_sigma M. The cartesian square in 3.2.1.9(ii) identifies c_omega M with the pullback f_M*alpha_M. Functoriality then gives -Id_sigma omega M=chi(f_M) alpha_M. Under pi_0(M)=0 the counit alpha_M is a quasi-isomorphism, so chi(f_M)=-alpha_M^{-1}. The types are M->sigma omega M on both sides. Without that condition, f_M is not the asserted short exact sequence and alpha need not be invertible; the printed qualification is necessary and sufficient here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.7-pushout-splitting-criterion	2026-11-18 18:48:00 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted splitting proof and fraction type check	If t*X splits for a quasi-isomorphism t:F->Fprime, naturality gives 0=chi(t*X)=(sigma t)chi(X); sigma t is invertible, hence chi(X)=0. Conversely the boundary of Id_F in Ext^0(F,F)->Ext^1(E,F) is chi(X), so vanishing gives a derived retraction r:M->F with ri=Id_F. Represent r by left fractions as M->Fprime<-F. Equality after composition with i is witnessed after a further denominator in the homotopy category. Absorb that denominator into q; then q i is homotopic to a quasi-isomorphism and hence is itself a quasi-isomorphism. Set t=q i. The pushout i*X has middle object M amalgamated-over-F M. The map [(m1,m2)]|->m1+m2 is well-defined and retracts its first M inclusion, so i*X splits. Its further pushout q*(i*X)=t*X also splits.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.7-pullback-splitting-criterion	2026-11-18 18:48:00 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted splitting proof and fraction type check	Dually, vanishing of chi(X) gives a derived section of M->E from the covariant Hom exact sequence. Right fractions represent it after a quasi-isomorphism from Eprime, and a further denominator makes the composite Eprime->M->E homotopic to a quasi-isomorphism; the composite s itself is therefore a quasi-isomorphism. The pullback X*s has the strict section e|->(q(e),e), so it splits. Conversely a split pullback gives chi(X)s=0 and invertibility of s implies chi(X)=0. This supplies the argument omitted as analogous in the printed proof.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.8-typed-fractions-and-footnote	2026-11-18 18:48:00 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted splitting proof and fraction type check	For t:F->Fprime, (sigma t)^{-1} chi(X) is a map E->sigma F. For s:Eprime->E, chi(Y)s^{-1} has the same source and target. The corresponding two primed equalities are type-consistent. If the common-codomain comparison of part b exists, homotopy gives rprime tprime=rsecond tsecond in the derived category, and equality of pushed-out Yoneda extensions gives equal boundary maps; composing by the inverse common denominator proves the required derived equality. The footnote follows from functoriality of sigma: it preserves identities, compositions and inverses in the derived category. Existence and necessity in the proposition, and the continuation of b-prime, await the next page; they are not claimed proved by this type check.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.8-a-extension-representation	2026-11-18 18:54:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted extension, adjunction and fraction proof	Represent u:E->sigma F by E--f->G<-s--sigma F with s a quasi-isomorphism. Since pi_0(sigma F)=0, pi_0(G)=0 and the exact sequence f_G is available. Let X=-f_G*f, represented by the pullback of f_G along -f. In the displayed diagram the kernel map to omega G is the identity. The natural map f_{sigma F}->f_G has components omega s, phi s and s. Put t=(omega s) beta_F. Both beta_F and omega s are quasi-isomorphisms, so t is a quasi-isomorphism; this uses preservation of weak equivalences by omega, not the unrestricted iterated-loop comparison corrected on p67. Naturality yields chi(X)=-chi(f_G)f and chi(f_G)s=(sigma omega s)chi(f_{sigma F}). Consequently (sigma t)^{-1}chi(X)=-(sigma beta_F)^{-1}chi(f_{sigma F})u. Lemma 3.2.3.6 gives chi(f_{sigma F})=-alpha_{sigma F}^{-1}. The adjunction triangle identity alpha_{sigma F}(sigma beta_F)=Id gives alpha_{sigma F}^{-1}=sigma beta_F. Thus the coefficient before the initial minus is -Id and the displayed expression equals u. All maps have source E and target sigma F. This proves the unparenthesized existence assertion a.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.8-a-dual-existence	2026-11-18 18:54:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted extension, adjunction and fraction proof	For the omitted parenthesized assertion, choose a right-fraction representation u=f s^{-1}, with a quasi-isomorphism s:Eprime->E and f:Eprime->sigma F. Put Y=-c_F*f. Lemma 3.2.3.6 gives chi(c_F)=-Id_{sigma F}, hence chi(Y)=f and chi(Y)s^{-1}=u. This verifies the omitted dual existence argument directly.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.8-bprime-sufficiency	2026-11-18 18:54:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted extension, adjunction and fraction proof	The continued condition uses rprime:D->Eprime and rsecond:D->Esecond with s_prime rprime homotopic to s_second rsecond and equal pulled-back Yoneda extensions. Naturality makes their boundary maps equal. The common composite is a quasi-isomorphism, so its inverse gives equality of the derived fractions. Necessity of the two comparison criteria still awaits the next page and is not asserted here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.8-b-necessity	2026-11-18 18:57:16 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted comparison proof and exact-sequence check	Left fractions give quasi-isomorphisms r1prime:Fprime->G1 and r1second:Fsecond->G1 with r1prime tprime homotopic to r1second tsecond. Put X1prime=r1prime*Xprime and X1second=r1second*Xsecond. Naturality gives chi(X1prime)=(sigma r1prime)chi(Xprime) and its second counterpart; the first printed identity omits chi on the right and is ill-typed without it. Equality of the original derived fractions and invertibility of their common denominator imply chi(X1prime)=chi(X1second). Their Baer difference delta in Yoneda Ext^1(E,G1) therefore has chi(delta)=0. Lemma 3.2.3.7 supplies a quasi-isomorphism q:G1->G such that q*delta is split; additivity means q*X1prime=q*X1second. Taking rprime=q r1prime and rsecond=q r1second gives both required comparison conditions. Conversely the common-pushout equality and naturality give (sigma rprime)chi(Xprime)=(sigma rsecond)chi(Xsecond); rprime tprime=rsecond tsecond then gives the displayed inverse calculation and equal original fractions.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.3.8-bprime-dual-necessity	2026-11-18 18:57:16 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted comparison proof and exact-sequence check	For the omitted dual proof, right fractions supply a common source D1 with quasi-isomorphisms r1prime:D1->Eprime and r1second:D1->Esecond and homotopic composites to E. Pull back Yprime and Ysecond to D1. The assumed equality of derived fractions implies equality of the resulting boundary maps. Their Baer difference has zero boundary. The pullback form of Lemma 3.2.3.7 gives a quasi-isomorphism q:D->D1 on which that difference splits. Thus rprime=r1prime q and rsecond=r1second q give equal pulled-back Yoneda classes and homotopic composites, proving necessity in b-prime. Sufficiency was checked on p80.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.4-middle-quasi-isomorphism	2026-11-18 18:57:16 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted comparison proof and exact-sequence check	In the displayed morphism of short exact sequences, the induced long exact homotopy sequences are natural. If the two endpoint vertical maps fprime and fsecond are quasi-isomorphisms, a diagram chase or the five lemma applied to each five-term segment shows that the middle map f is an isomorphism on every homotopy group, including degree zero via the terminating exact sequence. Thus the parenthetical assertion is valid. The definition of VT.(A) continues on the next page and is not yet treated as complete.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.4-localization-and-conservativity	2026-11-18 19:04:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted localization proof, explicit counterexample and index check	Tensoring a short exact sequence with the degreewise finite free cylinder Z^(Delta(1)) preserves exactness, so the stated homotopies are morphisms of short exact sequences. The earlier cylinder and path factorizations apply termwise: a map can be replaced by a monomorphism or epimorphism of short exact sequences, with an exact-sequence cokernel or kernel. Pushout along the former and pullback along the latter stay short exact by the nine lemma; if the relevant kernel/cokernel is termwise acyclic, the resulting map is again a quasi-isomorphism. These constructions give the same bilateral fraction and cancellation arguments as 3.1.8. The chi construction sends a quasi-isomorphism to a triangle isomorphism, because all three object maps are quasi-isomorphisms, and therefore descends to VT.(A). Essential surjectivity follows from the cone-extension construction verified on p77. If a roof in VT.(A) maps to an isomorphism of triangles, its numerator has endpoint quasi-isomorphisms after its denominator is inverted. The numerator itself is therefore a quasi-isomorphism, proving conservativity.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.2.4-explicit-nonfaithfulness-example	2026-11-18 19:04:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted localization proof, explicit counterexample and index check	Take the point topos and constant ring Z. Let X be the short exact sequence 0->Z --i-> Z direct-sum Z/2 --pi-> Z/4->0, with i(a)=(2a,-a mod 2) and pi(b,t)=b+2t mod 4. Let Y be 0->Z --j-> Z^2 --q-> Z direct-sum Z/2->0, with j(a)=(0,2a) and q(b,c)=(b,c mod 2). Both sequences are exact. Localizing short exact sequences at componentwise quasi-isomorphisms agrees with localizing their monomorphism arrows: quotient is unique, and the functorial mapping-cylinder replacement of any arrow is a monomorphism with a natural componentwise quasi-isomorphism back. Normalization thus computes these morphisms in the derived arrow category of abelian groups. For arrow objects i and j, the long exact derived-Hom sequence injects coker[Hom(Z,Z) direct-sum Hom(Z direct-sum Z/2,Z^2) -> Hom(Z,Z^2)] into Ext^1_arrow(i,j); the map is (a,b)|->j a-b i. Its image is exactly 2Z^2, since b annihilates the torsion summand. The map h(a)=(2a,-a) gives the nonzero class (0,1) in Z^2/2Z^2, hence a nonzero true-triangle morphism X->sigma Y. By construction its first two derived component maps are zero. Its third component is the connecting class of qh:Z->Z direct-sum Z/2; but qh=i extends to the identity on the middle object of X, so that connecting class is zero as well. The associated morphism of distinguished triangles is therefore zero. This explicit nonfaithfulness proves that chi need not be an equivalence, while being compatible with conservativity.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.1-two-simplicial-directions	2026-11-18 19:04:38 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted localization proof, explicit counterexample and index check	An object M of Simpl(A-Mod) has entries M_pq, with the horizontal index p carrying the simplicial A-module structure and the vertical index q the extra simplicial structure. Thus M_.q is an A-module and M_p. is a simplicial A_p-module. The constant-vertical functor sends L to M_pq=L_p with identity maps in q. Taking the diagonal gives (Delta M)_n=M_nn=L_n with the original structure maps, so the displayed composite is the identity. The phrase diagonal subobject is retained because the already reviewed section 1.1 explicitly defines that term for diagonal restriction; it is not a claim of a monomorphism in the bisimplicial category. The gamma tensor construction continues on p83.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.1.4-diagonal-cones	2026-11-18 19:08:34 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted diagonal, spectral-sequence and module type check	Diagonal restriction is evaluated degreewise and therefore preserves the pushouts defining both cones. In either extension of gamma to two simplicial directions, its (n,n) value is gamma_n, and the diagonal of the unit inclusion is the ordinary unit. Thus Delta(gamma_h E) and Delta(gamma_v E) both identify with gamma(Delta E), and both diagonal pushouts are C(Delta f), with the same signed quotient relation (-i_E,f). This equality does not decide the orientation convention questioned in I-Q006.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.1.5-spectral-sequence-factorization	2026-11-18 19:08:34 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted diagonal, spectral-sequence and module type check	The homology A-modules in the definition are vertical homology H_q^v(M), which retain the horizontal simplicial A-action. An arrow inverted in D_v gives isomorphisms of these A-modules; one inverted in D_hv gives quasi-isomorphisms, hence isomorphisms on H_p^h H_q^v. The first-quadrant normalized bicomplex has a finite filtration in each total degree, so E2 isomorphisms imply an isomorphism on total homology. The Eilenberg-Zilber comparison then makes the diagonal map a quasi-isomorphism. Therefore the composite A-Mods --Delta-> A-Mod -> D.(A) factors uniquely through D_hv; W_v is contained in W_hv, supplying D_v->D_hv. This is precisely the displayed commutative diagram. The prose factorization is read with its explicit i.e. diagram and is not construed as sending inverted maps to strict isomorphisms of unlocalized A-Mod.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.1-footnote-module-typing	2026-11-18 19:08:34 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted diagonal, spectral-sequence and module type check	An n-simplicial object of Lambda-Mod has a Lambda action compatible with every simplicial structure map. This is exactly a module over Lambda considered as an n-simplicial ring with all structure maps identities. Thus both formulations in the footnote agree.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.1.6-suspension	2026-11-18 19:16:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted suspension, localization, flatness and counterexample proof	Apply Delta C_v(f)=C(Delta f) to f:E->0. Since C_v(E->0)=sigma_v E and C(Delta E->0)=sigma Delta E, the equality is Delta sigma_v=sigma Delta. In the point topos over constant Z, a constant module Z has homology in degree 0 while its suspension has homology in degree 1, so the printed equality without the right-hand sigma is false. This argument does not resolve I-Q006 because both directional suspensions have the same diagonal.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.1-localizations-and-retraction	2026-11-18 19:16:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted suspension, localization, flatness and counterexample proof	Vertical normalization is the Dold-Puppe equivalence between simplicial A-modules and nonpositive cochain complexes of A-modules. The good truncation tau_{<=0}K -> K is a vertical weak equivalence when H^q(K)=0 for q>0, and a horizontal-vertical weak equivalence when each H^q(K) is horizontally acyclic for q>0. Good truncation preserves both weak classes, and its natural counit plus its identity on nonpositive complexes exhibits the respective full essential images after localization. A constant vertical object has vertical homology only in degree 0, so a horizontal quasi-isomorphism becomes an hv weak equivalence. The diagonal of a constant vertical object is the original A-module, giving the displayed retraction.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2-flat-counterexample-and-repair	2026-11-18 19:16:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted suspension, localization, flatness and counterexample proof	Take the point topos, constant simplicial R=k[epsilon]/(epsilon^2), and the cochain complex L with L^i=R for every integer i and d^i=multiplication by epsilon. Since epsilon^2=0 and ker(epsilon)=epsilon R=im(epsilon), L is acyclic and L->0 is a quasi-isomorphism; every term is free and its cohomology is bounded above. Put F=k=R/(epsilon) in degree 0. Then L tensor_R F has k in every degree and zero differential, whereas 0 derived-tensor_R F=0. Thus the printed flat-component condition alone is false even within its cohomologically bounded-above definition of D_v^-. For the added sufficient condition, a bounded-above flat complex is the filtered union of its bounded brutal truncations from below. Each bounded flat complex preserves tensor acyclicity by induction on its length using the split degreewise short exact sequence for the top term. Tensor commutes with filtered colimits, which are exact for module sheaves; consequently the union preserves tensor acyclicity and computes the claimed derived tensor with arbitrary F.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2-degreewise-flatness	2026-11-18 19:16:25 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted suspension, localization, flatness and counterexample proof	If every L_n is flat over A_n, exactness of tensor with L follows degreewise. Conversely, evaluation at n has a right adjoint: in simplicial degree p it is the finite product, over arrows p->n in Delta^op, of a given A_n-module restricted along A_p->A_n. Restriction and finite products are exact. At p=n the identity arrow contributes the given module as a direct factor. Applying exact tensor with L to this exact coinduction and then evaluation at n shows that tensor with L_n is exact on the identity direct factor. Hence each L_n is flat. This uses the exact right adjoint, not an unjustified exactness claim for extension of scalars.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2.1-flat-tensor-lemma	2026-11-18 19:21:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted flat-tensor proof and finite derived-tensor obstruction	For constant A=R, normalized simplicial modules are bounded-below chain complexes. If L is degreewise flat, N(L) has flat terms and is tensor-acyclicity preserving, since its finite brutal truncations and filtered colimit give the usual bounded-below homological argument. If E and F are flat, the acyclic bounded-below flat cone of N(f) has flat cycles inductively from its bottom degree; its exact sequences are pure, so tensoring it with any module, and then a bounded-below complex, is acyclic. Eilenberg-Zilber transfers this to simplicial tensors. In the general simplicial-ring case, each row of the standard free resolution is A^(X); its tensor with M is canonically Z^(X) tensor_Z M, and Z^(X) is degreewise free, so the upper horizontal map is a quasi-isomorphism on each row. In each fixed horizontal degree the vertical augmentation is a free resolution over A_p. If L_p is flat, or if E_p and F_p are flat, the constant-ring case makes the vertical arrows quasi-isomorphisms on each column. First-quadrant bicomplex spectral sequences then give a diagonal quasi-isomorphism for the lower map. This proves the lemma with precisely its two alternatives.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2.2-literal-localization-obstruction	2026-11-18 19:21:26 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted flat-tensor proof and finite derived-tensor obstruction	Under the literal p83-p84 localization at maps inducing horizontal quasi-isomorphisms on vertical homology, each H_p^h H_q^v factors through D_hv. For constant Z, let P be the simplicial free resolution with normalized chain complex [Z --2--> Z] in horizontal degrees 1,0, let M be constant Z/2, and let f:P->M be the augmentation. This is an hv weak equivalence when viewed in vertical degree 0. But P derived-tensor_v M = P tensor M has E2 groups Z/2 at (p,q)=(0,0),(1,0), whereas M derived-tensor_v M has E2 groups Z/2 at (0,0),(0,1). Thus these objects cannot be isomorphic under that literal localization. Check later context and author errata before deciding the smallest defensible repair to the definition or descent statement; do not silently replace the category by total quasi-isomorphisms.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2.6-direct-flat-resolution-construction	2026-11-18 19:25:39 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted cofinality, exactness and normalization proof	For every A-module P, the standard free bisimplicial resolution has a vertical augmentation to constant P which is a columnwise quasi-isomorphism. First-quadrant convergence and Eilenberg-Zilber make Delta F_A(P)->P a quasi-isomorphism. Its n-th component is free over A_n, hence flat. Any denominator P->E is therefore dominated by a flat denominator; common refinements and equalizers in the fraction category can also be refined this way, proving cofinality of flat denominators. Tensor sends quasi-isomorphisms between flat A-modules to quasi-isomorphisms by 3.3.2.1, and a flat L sends every quasi-isomorphism in the other argument to a quasi-isomorphism. The pro-system on pairs of flat denominators is thus essentially constant, defining the left derived tensor directly, computed by L tensor F or E tensor M when the corresponding resolution is flat. This verifies the universal-property construction independently of 3.3.2.5; its identification with the displayed intermediate D_hv composite is NOT certified, because I-Q007 remains open.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2.7-exactness	2026-11-18 19:25:39 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted cofinality, exactness and normalization proof	Fix a degreewise-flat resolution L->E and compute the directly constructed derived functor by L tensor -. It preserves quasi-isomorphisms by 3.3.2.1, commutes with gamma and sigma by associativity of degreewise tensor, and preserves the pushout defining a mapping cone because tensor is a left adjoint. It therefore sends cone triangles, their isomorphic images and distinguished triangles to distinguished triangles and commutes with suspension. No unbounded-flat-complex assertion is used.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.2.8-constant-ring-comparison	2026-11-18 19:25:39 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted cofinality, exactness and normalization proof	When the simplicial ring is constant A, normalization identifies a degreewise-flat simplicial resolution L with a bounded-below chain complex of flat A-modules. Eilenberg-Zilber identifies the normalized tensor with the total tensor of normalized complexes up to chain homotopy equivalence. Since that bounded-below flat complex computes the usual derived tensor, the directly constructed simplicial derived tensor agrees with the ordinary derived tensor over the same ring A. The scan uses A for both the constant simplicial ring and its value; no Lambda is introduced.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.3.1-direct-spectral-sequence-proof	2026-11-18 19:30:15 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted spectral-sequence, shuffle and coefficient type proof	Use the standard free vertical resolution F_A(E)->E and tensor it with F. Its vertical homology, in horizontal degree n, is Tor_q^{A_n}(E_n,F_n); the horizontal structure maps make this the simplicial module Tor_q^A(E,F). The first-quadrant bicomplex spectral sequence therefore has E2_{p,q}=H_p(Tor_q^A(E,F)). Its total homology equals the diagonal homology by Eilenberg-Zilber. The diagonal tensor equals Delta F_A(E) tensor_A F, and Delta F_A(E) is a flat simplicial resolution of E by p86, so the abutment is H_*(E derived-tensor_ell F). Functoriality follows from the functorial standard resolution. This proves the displayed spectral sequence directly without certifying the disputed intermediate D_hv descent of I-Q007. Indeed that question permits different E2 bidegrees under a horizontal quasi-isomorphism while preserving the abutment, which is consistent with functoriality on actual A-modules here.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.3.3.3.3-4-shuffle-balancing	2026-11-18 19:30:15 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	targeted spectral-sequence, shuffle and coefficient type proof	The shuffle map is a chain map with the graded Leibniz rule. Its associativity and Koszul symmetry make the A-tilde actions on L-tilde and M-tilde balanced, so it descends to L-tilde tensor_{A-tilde} M-tilde -> (L tensor_A M)-tilde. Sending two homology classes to the class of their shuffle is independent of cycle representatives: replacing a cycle by a boundary changes the product by a boundary by the Leibniz rule. The same associativity gives balancing over H_*(A), yielding (3.3.3.4), naturally in both variables. The prose names L,M,A refer explicitly to these differential graded objects, so their dropped tildes are harmless shorthand in that sentence.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
