ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.1.1.5-totalization-existence-obstruction	2026-11-18 20:18:24 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	existence counterexample, conditional spectral-sequence proof and normalization proof	The section assumes only that A is abelian (p94), while 1.2.1 on p6 defines the simple complex by direct sums along total-degree diagonals. Let A be finite-dimensional vector spaces over a field k and let X^(-n,n)=k for every n>=0, all other terms and all differentials zero. This is an allowed second-quadrant bicomplex, but its prescribed degree-zero simple term would be the countable direct sum of k, which is not an object of A. Therefore the unqualified functor integral:D_bullet^bullet(A)->D(A) cannot literally be supplied by that construction for arbitrary abelian A. This is an explicit existence counterexample, not a computation. The supplied b superscripts in two displays are not a justified repair: the scan has bullets, and i on arbitrary unbounded D(A) does not land in D_bullet^b(A). The minimal intended correction remains I-Q009.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.1.1.5-conditional-totalization-proof	2026-11-18 20:18:24 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	existence counterexample, conditional spectral-sequence proof and normalization proof	Under the additional sufficient assumption that A has exact countable filtered colimits, finite sums and those colimits form the required countable direct sums. Each good vertical truncation t_(q])X lies in 0<=vertical degree<=q, so its total diagonals are finite and its horizontal-after-vertical cohomology spectral sequence has finite filtrations in every degree. The hypotheses on H_vert(f) therefore make integral t_(q])f a quasi-isomorphism. The colimit over q identifies with the direct-sum totalization: each original bidegree stabilizes, and finite-support elements occur in some truncation. Exactness of the colimit makes its cohomology the colimit of the cohomologies, proving integral f is a quasi-isomorphism. Integral i=Id follows from p94-95. If the horizontal cohomology of H_vert^q(X) vanishes for q>N, the truncated spectral sequences have no total cohomology above N (horizontal degrees are <=0), and exact passage to the colimit gives the claimed bounded-above image. Conversely i sends D-minus into the bounded vertical-homology subcategory by the p95 formula. This is a conditional proof only; no absent hypothesis is treated as printed.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
