ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.1.2.2-4-quasiinverse-and-localization	2026-11-18 20:27:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	Dold-Kan equivalence proof, explicit existence counterexample and additive-case proof	For any abelian A, horizontal and vertical denormalization use finite biproducts indexed by finite sets of ordinal surjections. The two constructions commute by the canonical interchange of those finite sums, and their face/degeneracy maps commute. This identifies the two routes in the K square, inverse to the N square already checked on p96. N and K are therefore mutually quasi-inverse between mixed simplicial objects and the second-quadrant bicomplex category. The earlier 1.3.3(i) gives exactness. Applied in the horizontal simplicial direction, exact normalization commutes with vertical cohomology, and the Dold-Kan equivalence preserves and detects the horizontal quasi-isomorphisms on that cohomology. Hence N and K carry precisely the stated inverted classes to one another and induce the displayed equivalence Dmixt(A) <-> D_bullet^bullet(A). This does not require the disputed totalization functor.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.1.3.2-nonadditive-totalization-obstruction	2026-11-18 20:27:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	Dold-Kan equivalence proof, explicit existence counterexample and additive-case proof	There is an explicit obstruction even for a bounded input complex. Set A=B=finite-dimensional vector spaces over k=F_2 and F(V)=k[V], the free k-vector space on the finite underlying set, with F(f)[v]=[f(v)]. This is a well-defined functor to B. Let L have k in cochain degrees -1 and +1, zero elsewhere and zero differential. The axis embedding iL and finite Dold-Kan formulas give (KiL)_p^q=k^p direct-sum k^q for p,q>=0. Thus Y=F(KiL) has dimension 2^(p+q) in bidegree (p,q). For a simplicial or cosimplicial vector space, the split Dold-Kan decomposition gives dim Y_n=sum_(j=0)^n binom(n,j) dim (NY)_j; binomial inversion therefore gives dim (N_h N_v Y)_p^q=(2-1)^p(2-1)^q=1 in every bidegree. The prescribed direct-sum totalization would have, in total degree zero, one copy of k for every p=q>=0. That infinite-dimensional space is not an object of B. Consequently the arbitrary-functor formula (4.1.3.2), with only the printed abelian-category assumptions, is not always defined, even on bounded L. This is a proof by the finite Dold-Kan dimension formulas, not a numerical computation. Carry I-Q009 forward for a justified hypothesis repair after reading the surrounding degree discussion.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.1.3.2-additive-case	2026-11-18 20:27:06 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	Dold-Kan equivalence proof, explicit existence counterexample and additive-case proof	Every additive functor preserves finite biproducts and split idempotents, hence commutes with the finite denormalization formulas and the split normalization projectors; exactness is unnecessary. Therefore NFKi is naturally isomorphic to FNKi, hence to Fi. Since additive F preserves zero objects and zero morphisms, Fi remains supported on the two axes, with only one possible object in each total degree. Its simple complex thus exists in arbitrary abelian B and is the usual degreewise extension F(L), with differential F(d). This proves the printed additive-case assertion independently of I-Q009.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
