ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1-local-colimit	2026-11-18 20:39:44 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	explicit dimension proof/computation and local-colimit/flatness proofs	For a local inductive system in the stack of sheaves, form the sheafification of local representatives in the indexed objects, identifying representatives when they become equal after a local common refinement. Local filteredness gives local common targets for every finite collection and local equalizers for parallel transition maps. Compatible maps from these representatives to a sheaf E are exactly maps from the system to the trivial system E, proving the stated left-adjoint universal property. For finite products, represent finitely many sections over one local common stage; for equalizers, an equality in the limit becomes an equality after local refinement. Thus the comparison with each finite limit is locally surjective and locally injective, hence an isomorphism of sheaves. Terminal objects are handled by local nonemptiness. This proves finite-limit compatibility without assuming enough points or commuting arbitrary limits.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.2-flat-factorization	2026-11-18 20:39:44 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	explicit dimension proof/computation and local-colimit/flatness proofs	Work locally where E has a finite presentation A^m -> A^n -> E ->0. A map E->M is a finite tuple x in M^n satisfying the m relations R x=0. Regard R as the corresponding map of finite free right A-modules. Flatness identifies ker(R tensor M) with ker(R) tensor M. A section of this tensor product is locally a finite sum of elementary tensors: x_i=sum_j b_ij y_j, with each coefficient column b_j in ker(R). The coefficient matrix therefore defines E->A^t, and sending the jth basis vector to y_j defines A^t->M; their composite is f. This proves the stated local factorization through a finite free module. All finiteness and locality steps are explicit, and the right-module formulation also handles noncommutative A.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.1-local-Lazard	2026-11-18 20:39:44 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	explicit dimension proof/computation and local-colimit/flatness proofs	Let I consist locally of all finite free maps A^n->M and commuting transition matrices. It is locally nonempty (the zero map), and two objects have a common target by direct sum. For parallel maps alpha,beta:A^r->A^s over M, the map A^s->M factors through the finitely presented cokernel of alpha-beta. The proved lemma locally factors that cokernel map through a finite free module, giving a local transition that equalizes alpha,beta. Thus I is locally filtered. Its local colimit maps onto M because any local section comes from A->M. If an element in a finite free source maps to zero, the finitely presented quotient by that single relation again factors locally through a finite free source, killing that element at a later stage; hence the colimit map is injective. Conversely, tensoring with a local filtered colimit of finite free modules is the corresponding local filtered colimit of exact finite-free tensor functors. Tensor commutes with that colimit by its universal property, and the finite-limit compatibility just proved makes the local filtered colimit exact on modules. Therefore the resulting module is flat. This proves both directions of the theorem from the factorization lemma.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.3-index-category	2026-11-18 20:48:27 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	local filteredness, colimit and induction-start proofs	The category of bounded complexes with components of finite presentation mapping to X is locally nonempty, using zero. After restriction to a common base, two objects have a common target by their direct sum. For two parallel arrows alpha,beta over X, take their degreewise cokernel complex. Each component remains of finite presentation: a cokernel of a map between finitely presented modules is finitely presented, since the source has finitely many local generators and hence contributes finitely many relations. The resulting bounded complex still maps to X and equalizes the arrows. This proves local filteredness without using flatness or acyclicity of X.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.3-local-colimit	2026-11-18 20:48:27 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	local filteredness, colimit and induction-start proofs	A local section x of X^n is represented by a map from the two-term disk D^n(A), with A in degrees n,n+1 and identity differential: send the generators to x and dx. This is a bounded complex with components of finite presentation, proving local surjectivity of the colimit map in degree n. If a section r of a source E^n maps to zero in X^n, the disk map with images r,dr has zero composite in X. Its cokernel is again a bounded complex with components of finite presentation, maps to X, and kills r. Thus any zero relation vanishes at a later local stage, proving injectivity. Common-stage representatives handle two sections, so the colimit map is an isomorphism of complexes.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.3-cofinality-and-start	2026-11-18 20:48:27 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	local filteredness, colimit and induction-start proofs	If every bounded finite-presentation complex mapping to X locally factors through a bounded contractible finite-free complex, the full subcategory of such complexes is locally cofinal: common targets and equalizations in the already locally filtered index category can be followed by that factorization, and fullness keeps the resulting arrows. For X supported in [a,b], induction starts at b-a=0 because acyclicity then forces X=0. Any map E->X factors through the brutal quotient truncation of E in degrees <=b; this is a quotient map, not an asserted subcomplex inclusion, and it keeps the finite-presentation components. The degree-b map E^b->X^b satisfies the hypotheses of the preceding flat-factorization lemma. The construction following that application is printed on p101 and has not yet been read; the induction and proposition are not claimed fully proved at this page boundary.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.3-finite-top-replacement	2026-11-18 20:52:08 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	pullback, split-induction, truncation-lift and universal-property proofs	In the bounded acyclic flat complex X, ker(X^(b-1)->X^b) is flat: tensor the short exact sequence with an arbitrary right module, using Tor_1(-,X^b)=0 and flatness of X^(b-1). After factoring E^b->X^b as E^b->P->X^b with P finite free, replace X^b by P and X^(b-1) by the fiber product X^(b-1) times_(X^b) P; leave lower terms unchanged. This is still acyclic, and its new degree-(b-1) term is an extension of P by the flat kernel, hence flat. The map E lifts into it: in degree b use E^b->P; in degree b-1 use the pair (f^(b-1),E^(b-1)->E^b->P). Thus the printed reduction to finite-free X^b is justified by a factorization through a new complex, without claiming that the original X^b itself becomes finite free.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.1.3-bounded-induction	2026-11-18 20:52:08 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	pullback, split-induction, truncation-lift and universal-property proofs	A finite-free top term P allows the epimorphism X^(b-1)->P to split locally by lifting finitely many basis sections. The complex splits into the contractible disk P --Id--> P in degrees b-1,b and the remaining acyclic flat complex supported in [a,b-1]. Its top term is the flat kernel already identified. Apply induction to the projection of E into the remaining summand, giving a bounded contractible finite-free factorization Y. Adding the disk summand yields Y direct-sum D^(b-1)(P), again bounded contractible and componentwise finite free in [a,b], and factors the original map E->X. This proves case(a), including the implicit replacement described in the preceding event.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
