ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:I.4.2.2.5-perfect-amplitude	2026-11-18 21:11:55 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE second pass 2026-11-18	colimit calculation, reducedness counterexample and amplitude proofs	Locally represent a perfect X of perfect amplitude[a,b] by a bounded complex Q of finite projective A-modules in that interval. Each term in KiQ is a finite sum of finite projectives and hence a retract of a finite free module. Every functor preserves a specified retraction, even without additivity. Thus, if F sends finite free modules to finite free modules, the F-values of these terms are finite projective. The two normalized modules are direct summands of those values, and the finite-degree bound leaves finite total diagonals. Consequently F(Q) is locally a bounded finite-projective complex supported in[na,nb], giving the claimed perfect amplitude. This uses projectivity of direct summands; it does not incorrectly claim that normalization preserves freeness. Localization compatibility from4.2.2.2 justifies checking this locally.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
