ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.5	2026-11-19 01:24:28 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Put delta(x)=j2(x)-j1(x) mod I squared. Additivity is immediate and delta(a)=0 for a in A. The difference delta(xy)-j1(x)delta(y)-j1(y)delta(x) is (j2(x)-j1(x))(j2(y)-j1(y)), which belongs to I squared. Thus the displayed sign order 1 tensor x minus x tensor 1 defines an A-derivation for the left B-action as asserted.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.6	2026-11-19 01:27:51 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For an A-derivation delta:B->M, define phi:B tensor_A B->M by phi(b tensor c)=b delta(c). It is A-balanced and satisfies phi(zw)=mu(z)phi(w)+mu(w)phi(z), hence vanishes on I squared when restricted to I. It induces a B-linear map I/I squared->M sending 1 tensor x minus x tensor 1 to delta(x). Conversely composition with d is an A-derivation. If z=sum b tensor c is in I, then sum bc=0 and z=sum (b tensor 1)(1 tensor c minus c tensor 1), proving generation and uniqueness. These inverse constructions commute with restriction and sheafification, establishing the displayed natural bijection. This is an independent check of the construction; no claim is made to having read the cited EGA passage.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.7-8	2026-11-19 01:27:51 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	A commutative square with map f:B->B-prime gives the A-derivation b->d-prime(f(b)) into Omega(B-prime/A-prime), regarded as a B-module. The universal property gives the semilinear map and its B-prime-linear tensor adjoint (1.1.2.8). Precomposition on Hom sends a derivation delta-prime to delta-prime composed with f, which verifies the displayed restriction map and its directions. Identity and composition follow on the generators d(b).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.9.first-claim	2026-11-19 01:27:51 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For the pushout B-prime=B tensor_A A-prime and a B-prime-module M, an A-derivation delta:B->M extends uniquely to an A-prime-derivation by delta-prime(b tensor a-prime)=a-prime delta(b). A-balancing uses delta(a)=0; the Leibniz rule follows directly on pure tensors. Restriction and extension are inverse and natural in M, so the representing base-change map Omega(B/A) tensor_B B-prime -> Omega(B-prime/A-prime) is an isomorphism. No flatness is used. The second assertion concerning (1.1.2.10) is not certified here because its predicate continues on p117.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.10	2026-11-19 01:29:50 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For B-prime=B tensor_A A-prime, an A-derivation into a B-prime-module M is equivalent to an independent pair of A-derivations from B and A-prime. The extension is delta(b tensor a-prime)=a-prime delta_B(b)+b delta_A-prime(a-prime); balancing uses that both derivations vanish on A. The two summands of (1.1.2.10) represent these choices. The displayed canonical map is consequently an isomorphism, completing the assertion begun on p116. More generally a compatible family of derivations on a diagram of ring arrows extends uniquely over its inductive limit, so the same universal property verifies commutation with arbitrary inductive limits in the category of modules with varying base ring.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.12-13	2026-11-19 01:29:50 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Quotient Omega(C/A) by the C-submodule generated by d(b) for b in B. Maps from the quotient to M are exactly B-derivations C->M, so it is Omega(C/B), proving right exactness. For C=B[X], a derivation is determined independently by its restriction to B and its values on X; hence Omega(C/A) is (Omega(B/A) tensor_B C) direct-sum C^(X), with Omega(C/B)=C^(X). This proves injectivity of the first map and exactness of the corrected short sequence, sectionwise and after exact sheafification.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.2.13.printed-target	2026-11-19 01:29:50 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	refuted, with the refined statement's id: II.1.1.2.13.corrected	The printed last nonzero term Omega(C/A) is refuted already by A=k, B=k[t], C=k[t,x]. After tensoring with k(t,x), the putative exact sequence would have vector-space dimensions 1,2,2, which violate the alternating rank identity. Replacing the last term by Omega(C/B), of rank one, agrees with the preceding transitivity sequence and the printed sectionwise proof.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p117.footnote-1	2026-11-19 01:29:50 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Using I 4.2.1.3 as the stated local filtered presentation of a flat module M by finite free modules M_i, the symmetric algebra S_B(M) is the filtered inductive limit of polynomial algebras S_B(M_i). The short transitivity sequences for these polynomial algebras form a compatible system. Their first terms have limit Omega(B/A) tensor_B S_B(M), while both differential terms commute with the limit by the checked universal property. Exactness of filtered inductive limits of sheaves of modules therefore gives the footnote assertion. This checks the reduction, not a fresh proof of I 4.2.1.3.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.3.1-2	2026-11-19 01:31:40 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	An A-linear map h:M->I extends to a derivation on the tensor algebra by d(m1...mn)=sum_j m1...m_(j-1) h(mj) m_(j+1)...mn. The symmetric commutation relations have zero image because I is a module over the commutative symmetric algebra, so the derivation descends to S_A(M). Its restriction is h; the Leibniz rule forces this extension uniquely. Consequently Der_A(S_A(M),I)=Hom_A(M,I)=Hom_B(B tensor_A M,I). The representing isomorphism Omega(S_A(M)/A)->S_A(M) tensor_A M sends d(m) to 1 tensor m, which checks the printed direction and both tensor subscripts without a projectivity or flatness hypothesis.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p118.symmetric-algebra-functoriality	2026-11-19 01:31:40 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For f:A->A-prime and an A-linear u:M->M-prime, the induced ring map S_f(u) restricts to f on A and u on M, so the displayed square commutes. Under the checked differential identification, d(m) maps to d(u(m)) and thus 1 tensor m maps to 1 tensor u(m); S_A(M)-linearity gives s tensor m -> S_f(u)(s) tensor u(m). This is exactly the printed S_f(u) tensor_A u, followed by the canonical A-prime balancing implicit in its target.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.1.4.1	2026-11-19 01:31:40 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The inverse-image functor of a geometric morphism preserves finite limits and all colimits. It therefore preserves the tensor product presentation, the multiplication map B tensor_A B->B and its kernel I, as well as the image defining I squared and the quotient I/I squared. The resulting identification is f^{-1}(I/I squared)=(f^{-1}I)/(f^{-1}I)^2, which is Omega(f^{-1}B/f^{-1}A). The construction commutes with maps of ring arrows, proving the claimed canonical functorial isomorphism.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p119.simplicial-components	2026-11-19 01:33:54 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Evaluation at [n] in the simplicial topos preserves the limits and colimits involved in I/I squared, so the degree-n component is Omega(B_n/A_n). A simplex map u:[m]->[n] induces ring maps B_n->B_m and A_n->A_m; functoriality of differentials gives the printed direction from degree n to degree m. This checks all four diagram indices and shows why the first printed B^n/A^n is an index-placement error.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p119.ringed-target	2026-11-19 01:33:54 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For a morphism f:X->Y, f^{-1} has source Y and target X, so f^{-1}(O_Y)->O_X is the required ring arrow and defines both relative principal parts and differentials. With the printed f:X->X, an independent O_Y is outside the domain of f^{-1}. The corrected target Y is therefore necessary, as also witnessed by the adjacent square.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p119.stalks-and-square-types	2026-11-19 01:33:54 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Applying the inverse-image result of p118 to a point p:Set->T gives the asserted stalk compatibility. For the ringed square, u^* f^* and f-prime^* v^* both go from modules on Y to modules on X-prime. Thus the specified isomorphism in the note is correctly typed; the claimed consequence for diagram (1.1.4.4) is deferred until that diagram is read.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p120.coefficient-ring-types	2026-11-19 01:37:57 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For f-prime:X-prime->Y-prime, f-prime^{-1} has domain sheaves on Y-prime. Thus f-prime^{-1}(O_X-prime) is generally undefined, while f-prime^{-1}(O_Y-prime) gives the required A-prime. The chosen commutativity isomorphism identifies u^{-1}f^{-1}(O_Y) with f-prime^{-1}v^{-1}(O_Y). The ringed structure maps of f,u,v,f-prime then give the four sides A->B, A->A-prime, B->B-prime, A-prime->B-prime; their compatibility is exactly that of the specified ringed square.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p120.base-change-map	2026-11-19 01:37:57 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Inverse-image compatibility identifies Omega(B/A) with u^{-1}Omega(X/Y). Tensoring with B-prime=O_X-prime identifies the source Omega(B/A) tensor_B B-prime with u^*Omega(X/Y). Functoriality of differentials for the coefficient-ring square supplies (1.1.4.3). If that ring square is a pushout, the universal derivation property gives the base-change isomorphism. No flatness assumption or geometric cartesian-square assertion is needed.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p120.transitivity	2026-11-19 01:37:57 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	On X take A=f^{-1}g^{-1}(O_Z), B=f^{-1}(O_Y), C=O_X. The already checked transitivity sequence Omega(B/A) tensor_B C -> Omega(C/A) -> Omega(C/B) -> 0 identifies, using inverse-image compatibility, with f^*Omega(Y/Z) -> Omega(X/Z) -> Omega(X/Y) -> 0. This verifies all three relative indices, the pullback star, and the terminal zero; it does not assert injectivity at the first term.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p121.standard-resolution-recursion	2026-11-19 01:40:08 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Let F_A be the free A-algebra functor on objects of the topos, U the forgetful functor, and G=F_A U. The standard augmented resolution has P_n=G^{n+1}(B), yielding P_0=A[B] and P_n=A[P_{n-1}] for n>0. Faces insert the counit, degeneracies insert the comultiplication F_A eta U; the adjunction identities give the simplicial identities. After U, the unit gives an extra degeneracy of the augmented simplicial object. Its contraction proves the augmentation is a weak equivalence; for the underlying simplicial abelian group this is the claimed quasi-isomorphism. The extra degeneracy itself is a set map and is not asserted to be additive.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p121.recursive-ring-square	2026-11-19 01:40:08 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Given alpha:A->A-prime and beta:B->B-prime commuting with structure maps, construct beta_n inductively: a coefficient a maps to alpha(a), and a free generator [x] maps to [beta_{n-1}(x)]. In the printed square both paths send every polynomial sum a_m times monomial(x) to sum alpha(a_m) times monomial(beta_{n-1}(x)); hence they agree and determine the diagonal. The four corner coefficient rings and all n-1 indices are correctly typed. Identity and composition follow on generators, and evaluation/adjunction naturality gives the augmented simplicial functoriality diagram.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p121.degree-index	2026-11-19 01:40:08 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	B was introduced as an ordinary A-algebra, so B_n is not defined in this paragraph. The recursive square has source A[P_A(B)_{n-1}]=P_A(B)_n and target A-prime[P_A-prime(B-prime)_{n-1}]=P_A-prime(B-prime)_n. Thus moving n outside the parentheses is forced, not a stylistic index convention.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p122.filtered-and-local-limits	2026-11-19 01:42:01 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For a filtered system (A_i->B_i), each polynomial uses finitely many coefficients and generators, so it comes from a common stage, and equality is detected after passing to a later common stage. Thus colim A_i[B_i] identifies with (colim A_i)[colim B_i]. Repeating this argument gives the resolution degree by degree, compatibly with evaluation and free-generator maps. For a locally filtered system over the topos, the same finite lifting and equality arguments hold after a common cover; associated sheaves identify the result. The use of locally filtered here agrees with the category described in the proof of I 4.2.1.3 on the previously reviewed p100; that prior proposition is not reproved by this check.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p122.inverse-image-and-associated-sheaf	2026-11-19 01:42:01 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	A geometric inverse image preserves finite products and all colimits. The free algebra is formed from finite strings/monomials, coproducts and the algebraic relations, so f^{-1}(A[S]) identifies naturally with f^{-1}(A)[f^{-1}(S)]. Recursion gives (1.2.1.4), and naturality preserves faces, degeneracies and augmentation. The associated-sheaf functor is likewise left exact and colimit preserving, giving (1.2.1.5) by the same construction.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p122.bisimplicial-and-diagonal-types	2026-11-19 01:42:01 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For simplicial A->B, forming the free resolution in Simpl(T) gives components P_{A_n}(B_n)_m. Fixing m gives the printed A-algebra row P_A(B)_m; fixing n gives the simplicial A_n-algebra column P_{A_n}(B_n). The original A and B are constant in the new vertical resolution direction. Taking diagonal n=m yields the three displayed ring maps A->P_A^Delta(B)->B and A->B, whose composite is the original structure map by augmentation compatibility. This checks the triangle, without pre-empting later claims about its augmentation.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p123.diagonal-quasi-isomorphism	2026-11-19 01:46:27 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For the bisimplicial underlying additive object P, take normalization in both nonnegative directions. Each vertical augmented column is the free resolution of B_n already checked on p121, hence has vertical homology B_n in degree zero and zero in positive degrees. The first-quadrant spectral sequence therefore identifies the homology of the total normalized complex with that of B. Each total degree has finitely many summands, so this use has no unbounded-totalization convergence issue. Applying the Eilenberg--Zilber comparison cited by the text gives the quasi-isomorphism from the diagonal to B. Degree n of the diagonal is P_{A_n}(B_n)_n, free over A_n by the recursive construction. This checks the application, not a new proof of the general Eilenberg--Zilber theorem.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p123.cotangent-functoriality-and-adjunction	2026-11-19 01:46:27 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Write phi:P->P-prime and beta:B->B-prime for the maps induced by the commutative ring square, with epsilon-prime phi=beta epsilon. The universal derivation gives the P-linear map Omega(P/A)->Omega(P-prime/A-prime), dp |-> d(phi(p)). The map on cotangent complexes sends dp tensor b to d(phi(p)) tensor beta(b); augmentation compatibility makes it balanced over P. It commutes with faces, degeneracies and composition by naturality of universal derivations and the resolution. Extension/restriction-of-scalars adjunction gives the displayed B-prime-linear map L_(B/A) tensor_B B-prime -> L_(B-prime/A-prime), without a flatness assumption or an assertion that it is an isomorphism.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p124.free-components-and-functoriality	2026-11-19 01:48:32 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	With S=P_A(B)_(n-1), P_n=A[S] and Omega(P_n/A)=P_n tensor_A A^(S), with basis dX_s. Tensoring by the augmentation gives (L_(B/A))_n=B tensor_A A^(S). The convention S=B at n=0 gives the same formula. For a square (A,B)->(A-prime,B-prime), the recursive map sends each generator X_s to X_(phi_(n-1)(s)), so the cotangent map sends b tensor e_s to beta(b) tensor e_(phi_(n-1)(s)). This proves both descriptions, including the equivalent free-Z-module version.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p124.missing-n-refutation	2026-11-19 01:48:32 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	refuted, with the refined statement's id	The printed domain P_A(B)_(-1)=B cannot induce the stated degree-n free-module map for general n: its generators must be indexed by P_A(B)_(n-1). At n=1 and A=B=Z, P_0 is the polynomial ring on the underlying set of Z; the basis element indexed by X_0+X_1 in P_0 requires the map P_0->P-prime_0, and is not indexed by an element of B. Thus restore n-1 in the corrected statement; refined statement II.p124.free-components-and-functoriality.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.p124.three-compatibilities	2026-11-19 01:48:32 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The filtered/local inductive-limit comparison follows degreewise by commuting the standard resolution, the module of differentials and the augmentation tensor product with the indicated limits. For the local version the construction and its equalities are local and sheafify, as on p122. A geometric inverse image, and likewise associated sheaf, preserves finite products and colimits; therefore it preserves the free resolution, the generators-and-relations construction of differentials and the balanced tensor product. Applying these facts to Omega(P/A) tensor_P B gives the two further comparisons, naturally in the ring map. The reference (1.1.4.1) here is the inverse-image formula on p118; the separate duplicated label on p119 remains tracked in Q014.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.4.2	2026-11-19 01:50:48 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For a B-module M, a map H0(L)->M is a map Omega(P0/A) tensor_(P0) B -> M equalizing the two face-induced maps from degree one. By universal derivations this is a derivation P0->M equalizing P1=>P0. Since B is their coequalizer as an A-algebra, these are exactly Der_A(B,M)=Hom_B(Omega(B/A),M). Yoneda identifies H0(L) with Omega(B/A), and the identification is induced by the augmentation.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.4.3	2026-11-19 01:50:48 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The cotangent complex is in nonnegative homological degrees and M is in degree zero. The degree-zero derived Hom is therefore Hom_B(H0(L),M), by the standard truncation property (or the degree-zero cycles of Hom into an injective resolution). Combining the preceding checked identification with the universal property of Omega gives Ext^0_B(L,M)=Der_A(B,M), naturally in M.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.4.4	2026-11-19 01:50:48 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Use the cited local Lazard--Deligne presentation of flat E by free modules. Symmetric algebra and the previously checked cotangent construction commute with this local inductive system; local exactness lets the claimed quasi-isomorphism be checked on the free stages. For B=A[X], the free-algebra adjunction supplies a split extra degeneracy, hence P_A(B)->B is a simplicial A-algebra homotopy equivalence. Applying the functor Omega(-/A) preserves the simplicial homotopy identities, so Omega(P/A)->Omega(B/A) is a quasi-isomorphism. Each Omega(P_n/A) is a free P_n-module. Thus tensoring the quasi-isomorphism P->B with the flat simplicial P-module Omega(P/A) gives a quasi-isomorphism Omega(P/A)->Omega(P/A) tensor_P B. The augmentation triangle and two-out-of-three prove the final assertion. This is the use of I 3.3.4.6, whose statement and unit argument on the already reviewed p92 were reread; no new proof of the general local Lazard--Deligne theorem is claimed.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.5.flatness	2026-11-19 01:54:07 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	In degree n, the diagonal resolution is P_(A_n)(B_n)_n, a polynomial A_n-algebra. Its differentials are free over that algebra; tensoring with B_n gives a free, hence flat, B_n-module. If A and B are constant simplicial rings, the resolution is constant in the horizontal direction, so taking the diagonal recovers the previously defined ordinary cotangent complex. Restriction of the bisimplicial augmentation consequently has the stated source and target.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.5.3	2026-11-19 01:54:07 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For each fixed horizontal degree n, the hypothesis B_n is a symmetric algebra on a flat A_n-module lets Proposition 1.2.4.4 identify the vertical column augmentation with a quasi-isomorphism. Normalize and take its mapping cone: all vertical-column homology vanishes. The first-quadrant bicomplex filtration converges because each total degree has only finitely many terms; its total complex is acyclic. Eilenberg--Zilber compares the total complex with the diagonal, so the diagonal augmentation is a quasi-isomorphism. This checks the applicability and convergence of the cited results, not a new proof of the general Eilenberg--Zilber theorem.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.2a.Whitehead	2026-11-19 01:56:39 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The base case n=-1 is B->B prime, a quasi-isomorphism by hypothesis. For the induction step, the map P_A(B)_(n-1)->P_Aprime(Bprime)_(n-1) is a map of simplicial rings and therefore of their additive simplicial abelian sheaves. Consequently hypothesis (ii) of I 2.2.2, reread on p25, applies to Corollary I 2.2.3 on p26; no assumption that the topos has enough points is needed. The induced map of free abelian sheaves is a quasi-isomorphism.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.2a.symmetric-functor	2026-11-19 01:56:39 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Both sides of (*) are degreewise free abelian sheaves. Their normalized complexes are bounded above in the cohomological convention (degrees <=0) and flat termwise, since normalized terms are direct summands. The full symmetric-algebra functor commutes with local inductive limits, being assembled from finite tensor products, coinvariants and direct sums. Lemma I 4.2.2.1 on p102 therefore shows that S_Z(*) is a quasi-isomorphism. Its nonpositive case involves ordinary simplicial normalization, so the unbounded two-sided totalization concern in I-Q009 is not used. The printed I 4.2.2.5 is the Tor-amplitude proposition, and is a wrong cross-reference; corrected French/English cite 4.2.2.1. This verifies only the portion of the induction printed on p127.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.2a.completion	2026-11-19 01:59:10 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Let Q and Q prime be the two polynomial Z-algebras in the final map on p127. They are degreewise free over Z, and Q->Q prime is a quasi-isomorphism. Thus the ordinary products A tensor_Z Q and A prime tensor_Z Q prime compute the derived products, so the two tensor factors can be changed through quasi-isomorphisms; these are the horizontal and vertical maps of (1.2.1.2.1). Their composite is (i)_n. For (iii)_n, formula (1.2.3.3.1) is B tensor_Z Z^(P_A(B)_(n-1)) and its primed counterpart: the free-abelian factors are flat, their map is a quasi-isomorphism by the checked Whitehead application, and B->B prime is a quasi-isomorphism. The same derived-tensor argument applies. For (ii)_n replace B by the already checked P_A(B)_n and use the universal free basis for differentials. The cited I 3.3.5.2 and I 3.3.2.6 have exactly these flat-representative properties.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.2b	2026-11-19 01:59:10 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The rowwise quasi-isomorphism of L gives a quasi-isomorphism on the diagonal by the first-quadrant bicomplex spectral sequence and Eilenberg--Zilber, as on p126. Since L^Delta is degreewise free over B, the map L^Delta -> L^Delta tensor_B B prime is a quasi-isomorphism for the quasi-isomorphism B->B prime, by I 3.3.4.6. Its composite with the base-change comparison is the first diagonal map; two-out-of-three proves the resp. variant.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.3-and-1.2.6.4	2026-11-19 01:59:10 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Degreewise freeness of B over A implies both that L^Delta->Omega(B/A) is a quasi-isomorphism (1.2.5.3) and that Omega(B/A) is degreewise free over B. Both sides therefore compute their derived base changes along B->B prime, so tensoring this comparison with B prime preserves the quasi-isomorphism. The span Omega(B/A) tensor_B B prime <- L^Delta tensor_B B prime -> L^Delta_(Bprime/Aprime) has both arrows quasi-isomorphisms, giving the canonical isomorphism in D.(B prime). This proves the necessity of Delta in the conclusion and the sufficiency of the two weaker hypotheses in Remark 1.2.6.4. The latter are exactly the only uses of degreewise freeness in the argument.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.5	2026-11-19 02:02:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	In the displayed square both vertical augmentations are quasi-isomorphisms by 1.2.5.3 because B and B prime are free degreewise over their respective bases. The top arrow is a quasi-isomorphism by 1.2.6.2 b), so two-out-of-three gives the bottom arrow. For the resp. arrow, Omega(B/A) is free degreewise over B; hence base change along the quasi-isomorphism B->B prime gives a quasi-isomorphism Omega(B/A)->Omega(B/A) tensor_B B prime by Kunneth. Its composite with the resp. arrow is the bottom arrow, and two-out-of-three applies again.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.6.localization	2026-11-19 02:02:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For a quasi-isomorphism C->C prime over B, 1.2.6.2 b) with A fixed gives a quasi-isomorphism L^Delta(C/A) tensor_C C prime -> L^Delta(C prime/A). Both sides are free degreewise as C prime-modules, so their tensor with B computes derived tensor and preserves this quasi-isomorphism. Tensor associativity identifies the result with the map assigned by (*). Thus (*) sends every inverted map to an isomorphism in D.(B), and the universal property of localization gives (**).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.6.augmentation	2026-11-19 02:02:36 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The augmentation L^Delta(C/A)->Omega(C/A) of 1.2.5.2 is functorial in the A-algebra C. Tensoring it with B through C->B gives (***) naturally on A-Alg/B. The target functor is not asserted here to preserve all quasi-isomorphisms; the localization claim on this page belongs to the source functor in (**).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.6.left-derived	2026-11-19 02:05:05 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For each quasi-isomorphism C prime -> C with C prime fibrant over B, the canonical resolution P_A^Delta(C prime)->C prime is again an admissible quasi-isomorphism: it is surjective degreewise and hence fibrant, so its composite to B is fibrant. These replacements refine the indexing system by algebras free degreewise over A. On that subsystem 1.2.5.3 compares differentials with the diagonal cotangent complex, and 1.2.6.5 makes the transition maps quasi-isomorphisms. Tensoring the comparisons with B is justified by degreewise freeness of both differential and cotangent modules. The common value is L^Delta(C/A) tensor_C B by 1.2.6.2. Thus the pro-object is essentially constant and the cited criterion I 1.4.4 yields the left derived functor. The already reviewed definitions on pp14 and62 were reread; retained crops of their notation and relative category were inspected again.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.7.dual-number-functor	2026-11-19 02:05:05 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	As an underlying simplicial abelian sheaf, the square-zero algebra B plus M is the direct sum B direct-sum M. For a quasi-isomorphism M->M prime, the induced map is id_B direct-sum f. Normalization preserves finite sums and its homology is the direct sum of the two homologies. Hence the induced algebra map is a quasi-isomorphism and the construction descends to the localized categories.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.7.ordinary-adjunction-star	2026-11-19 02:05:05 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Fix the structure map g:C->B. A map of A-algebras over B from C to B plus F has the form c |-> (g(c),d(c)). The square-zero multiplication says exactly d(cc prime)=g(c)d(c prime)+g(c prime)d(c), with d zero on A. Thus these maps are A-derivations C->F, represented by Hom_C(Omega(C/A),F), which tensor-Hom adjunction identifies with Hom_B(Omega(C/A) tensor_C B,F). These assignments commute with the simplicial operators and with maps in C,F, proving the printed canonical functorial bijection (*). This checks the opening step, not the still-continuing derived adjunction proof.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.7.homotopy-adjunction	2026-11-19 02:06:52 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The ordinary adjunction (*) is compatible with path objects: the relative path object of B plus F over B is the pullback of (B plus F)^{Delta1} over B^{Delta1} along constant paths, hence B plus F^{Delta1}. Applying (*) to F^{Delta1} identifies homotopies with their two endpoint maps on both sides. Passing to the equivalence relation generated by homotopy therefore gives the bijection (**).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.6.7.comparison-chain	2026-11-19 02:06:52 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Since the pro-system Omega(C/A) tensor_C B over fibrant quasi-isomorphisms C->B is essentially constant with value L^Delta(B/A), the definition of Hom from a pro-object gives the first displayed inductive limit. The bilateral fraction calculus for B-modules gives the second limit over target quasi-isomorphisms M->F. The two indexing categories are independent, so their iterated inductive limits commute. Apply (**) termwise; the relative right-fraction theorem I 3.1.8 (ii) prime then computes the inner C-limit as Hom_D(A-Alg/B)(B,B plus F). All maps B plus M -> B plus F are quasi-isomorphisms by the preceding page, so the outer diagram is naturally isomorphic to the constant Hom set with F=M. Its index category is nonempty and connected (identity M is initial), yielding the last isomorphism. Every comparison is natural in M. This completes the proof of Proposition 1.2.6.7 and identifies the correct citation in the third line.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.7.2	2026-11-19 02:09:13 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Inverse image identifies u^{-1}L_{X/Y} with the cotangent complex for u^{-1}O_X over u^{-1}f^{-1}O_Y by 1.2.3.5. The ring square induced by the given topos square then gives the map after tensoring over u^{-1}O_X with O_Xprime, exactly u^*L_{X/Y}->L_{Xprime/Yprime} by 1.2.3.2 prime. An explicit name for the lower topos arrow is unnecessary to define that square or its induced ring maps.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.7.transitivity-cocycle	2026-11-19 02:09:13 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For two successive squares, identify u_prime^*u^* with (u u_prime)^* by inverse-image composition and tensor associativity. At each stage of the canonical polynomial resolution the functorial map sends a generator to the generator indexed by its image. The composite of two such assignments equals the assignment for the composite ring map. Functoriality of differentials and associativity of scalar extension give the same equality for the induced cotangent maps under the canonical pullback identification. For three squares both parenthesizations send every generator and coefficient through the same three ring maps; the canonical inverse-image and tensor associators agree by their universal properties. This supplies the transitivity isomorphism and its cocycle compatibility left to the reader.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.1.2.7.3-and-1.2.7.4	2026-11-19 02:09:13 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The augmentation of the canonical algebra resolution is natural in every ring map, and taking its universal differential commutes with the map of resolutions. Extending scalars therefore gives the displayed commuting augmentation square, whose bottom map is the differential comparison of 1.1.4.3. Its H_0 identification is the already checked 1.2.4.2 applied to O_X over f^{-1}O_Y, so H_0(L_{X/Y}) is Omega^1_{X/Y}.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.1.3-and-2.1.1.4	2026-11-19 02:13:11 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	In degree n, Q_n is a free polynomial algebra over P_n. Its differentials over A_n decompose into Omega(P_n/A_n) tensor Q_n and the free Q_n-module on the polynomial generators. Thus the first summand embeds and the quotient is Omega(Q_n/P_n), giving the displayed short exact sequence. This quotient is flat, so Tor_1 over Q_n with C_n vanishes and scalar extension preserves the injection as well as right exactness. These natural maps commute with simplicial operators, yielding the short exact sequence of simplicial C-modules in (2.1.1.4). The last denominator P on p133 also agrees with the necessary correction of the repeated A in the cited p117 formula (1.1.2.13).		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.1.functoriality	2026-11-19 02:13:11 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	A commutative map of triples A->B->C and A-prime->B-prime->C-prime induces the map P_A^Delta(B)->P_A-prime^Delta(B-prime) by functoriality of the standard free resolution. Together with C->C-prime it induces Q=P_P^Delta(C)->Q-prime. Applying universal differentials sends d(p) to d(image(p)); hence both maps in the exact sequence commute with the induced semilinear maps. Tensoring along Q->C and Q-prime->C-prime gives the required map of exact sequences. Identity and composition follow on every free generator. The category of true triangles cited at I 3.2.4 is indeed the localization of short exact sequences, as reread on pp81-82; no change of reference or terminology is necessary.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.1.6-prime-and-footnote	2026-11-19 02:15:34 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Each degree of P is a free algebra over A, and Q is a free algebra over P; hence Q is also free over A. The three differential modules in (2.1.1.3) are degreewise free, so their scalar extensions to C are degreewise flat C-modules. In particular the quotient remains flat and tensoring the short exact sequence with C-prime preserves its injection. The semilinear map (2.1.1.6), viewed by restriction as a C-module map, is adjoint to the C-prime-linear map (2.1.1.6) prime. Thus both source and target in that formula really are exact sequences, as the footnote requires.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.2.diagram-and-vertical-maps	2026-11-19 02:15:34 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Naturality of the augmentation L^Delta->Omega^1 in both ring variables makes both squares of (*) commute. The lower row is the transitivity sequence already checked on p133. Proposition 1.2.5.3, reread on p126, applies to Q over P and to P over A because both are degreewise free polynomial algebras (free modules of generators satisfy its flatness hypothesis). It gives exactly the right vertical quasi-isomorphism and L^Delta_{P/A}->Omega^1_{P/A} asserted here. Extension by Q and the remaining comparison needed for the distinguished triangle continue on p135; they are not certified by this partial proof event.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.2.star-to-double-star	2026-11-19 02:17:56 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The left augmentation in (*) is the quasi-isomorphism L^Delta_{P/A}->Omega^1_{P/A} tensored with the degreewise free P-algebra Q, so the Kunneth/derived-scalar-extension result I 3.3.4.2 applies. Q is degreewise free over A by composition of polynomial algebra constructions, so 1.2.5.3 also gives the middle augmentation. Together with the right augmentation checked on p134 all three vertical maps are quasi-isomorphisms. All six modules are degreewise flat over Q: the upper ones by flatness of cotangent complexes and extension of scalars, the lower ones by polynomial freeness. Ordinary scalar extension on these flat simplicial modules computes derived scalar extension, and therefore preserves their quasi-isomorphisms on tensoring with C. This justifies (**) without assuming C is flat over Q. The cited I 3.3.4.2 was reread on p90.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.2.triple-star-and-triangle	2026-11-19 02:17:56 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	The augmentations P->B and Q->C are quasi-isomorphisms. Apply 1.2.6.2(b), reread on p127, to the pairs (A,P)->(A,B), (A,Q)->(A,C), and (P,Q)->(B,C). The identity of A in the first two pairs is also a quasi-isomorphism. These give the three vertical maps of (***); in the first column the comparison is additionally extended from B to C, which preserves it because both B-modules are degreewise flat. Tensor associativity identifies its upper object with L^Delta_{P/A} tensor_P C. The lower short exact sequence of (**) defines a distinguished triangle. Its canonical objectwise quasi-isomorphisms through the common upper row of (**) and (***) transport this triangle to the three cotangent objects in the proposition, with the displayed degree-zero arrows. All maps and their inverses in the derived category are functorial, giving the stated canonical functorial isomorphism. For constant simplicial rings the identification in 1.2.5.1 removes Delta and gives exactly the final triangle.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.1.2.H0-and-2.1.3	2026-11-19 02:22:14 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For constant rings, the cotangent complexes are connective. H_0(L_{B/A} tensor_B^L C)=H_0(L_{B/A}) tensor_B C, and H_0(L)=Omega^1 by 1.2.4.2. Therefore the end of the transitivity long exact sequence is precisely Omega^1_{B/A} tensor_B C -> Omega^1_{C/A} -> Omega^1_{C/B} -> 0, as in 1.1.2.12. For Proposition 2.1.3, apply 1.2.6.2(b) to all three ring pairs in the vertical quasi-isomorphism diagram. Degreewise flatness makes ordinary scalar extension on the displayed cotangent objects compute derived extension. Thus each of the three components is an isomorphism in the derived category; 2.1.2 and the true-triangle criterion I 3.2.4 give the assertion for the adjoint form as well.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
