ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.3.3.iii	2026-11-19 02:50:11 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	For localization maps u and v, put Y=v^{-1}(C^*) and X=u^{-1}(Y). A map f:A->C-prime inverting X first factors over B because X contains u^{-1}(B^*). Locally b=x^{-1}a; if v(b) is a unit, vu(a)=vu(x)v(b) is a unit, so a belongs to X and the factor over B inverts Y. It therefore factors uniquely over C. Conversely, if vu is a localization and u is an epimorphism, a map g:B->C-prime inverting Y gives a unique bar-g:C->C-prime with bar-g vu=gu. Cancelling u yields bar-g v=g, confirming the printed v is wrong. Uniqueness follows since vu, hence v, is an epimorphism. This proves both assertions.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
ILLUSIE_I_CHECK:II.2.3.3.iv	2026-11-19 02:50:11 UTC	GPT-6 Astra Pro; effort not exposed; ILLUSIE continuation 2026-11-19	targeted proof check	Units can be represented by the finite-limit object of pairs (b,c) with bc=1; projection onto b is a monomorphism because inverses are unique. Thus both f_* and the left-exact f^* commute with units. Adjunction identifies maps from f^*A inverting f^*X with maps from A to f_*B-prime inverting X, hence with maps from B to f_*B-prime and finally from f^*B to B-prime. This proves preservation of localization. Conversely let X=u^{-1}(B^*). The comparison X^{-1}A->B becomes an isomorphism after f^*: f^*X is the full inverse image of units for the localization f^*u. A faithful functor reflects monomorphisms and epimorphisms; applying this on the underlying topoi, which are balanced, shows it reflects isomorphisms. Therefore the comparison was an isomorphism. The proof works for both the printed endomorphism and arbitrary distinct source and target topoi; it does not alone establish an obligatory prime on p144.		french_school_pro_checks_20261117_work/returns/ILLUSIE_I_CHECK_checkpoint_15_20261119T0530Z_unzipped/CHECK_EVENTS.tsv	
